(1178, 6, '2026-09-04 18:31:25', '2026-09-04 18:31:25', '\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">As Americans increasingly expect 911 to provide help during mental health emergencies, especially suicide calls, a new IAED protocol is available to give Emergency Dispatchers the structure and tools for responding to callers in crisis </h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>September 4, 2026 (Salt Lake City, UT) </strong>— A new national survey conducted by the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch (IAED) reveals that Americans place significant trust in 911 Emergency Dispatchers during suicide-related and mental health emergencies and overwhelmingly expect them to have specialized training to handle these life-and-death calls.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">According to the survey of more than 1,500 Americans, 89% trust 911 Emergency Dispatchers to provide guidance during a suicide-related emergency, while 73% believe dispatchers actually receive specialized training to manage suicide risk calls. At the same time, 87% support providing Emergency Dispatchers with specialized training for suicide and mental health crisis situations.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The findings highlight a troubling disconnect between public expectations and reality.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Recognizing the urgent need for a consistent, evidence-based approach for handling these types of emergency calls, the IAED developed Protocol 41: Caller in Crisis (1st Party). Protocol 41 brings a new level of preparedness to emergency communications centers by providing IAED-certified Emergency Dispatchers with a structured calltaking protocol specifically for callers in crisis. With instructions for separating a caller from the means to do self-harm, and the Emotional Control Tool—a first-of-its-kind Pre-Arrival Instruction that gives Emergency Dispatchers mental state-specific techniques to deescalate situations where callers who are in personal crisis are overwhelmed by emotional or psychological distress—Protocol 41 enables dispatchers to offer immediate support, guide callers toward safety, and remain a calming presence during moments of personal crisis.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">&#8220;As this survey demonstrates, Americans clearly view 911 Emergency Dispatchers as a trusted source of guidance during some of life&#8217;s most critical moments,&#8221; said Jerry Overton, IAED President. “The public expects dispatchers to be prepared for mental health emergencies, and Protocol 41 equips them with the right tools and training to meet those expectations.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Taking emergency calls from callers in crisis requires specialized skills and evidence-based procedures that many emergency communication professionals have not historically received through standard training programs. To help close this gap, the IAED developed Protocol 41: Caller in Crisis (1st Party Only), a specialized emergency communications protocol—the only one in the industry—designed to help Emergency Dispatchers effectively identify, support, and assist first party callers experiencing suicidal ideation, emotional distress, or other behavioral health crises.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Released in 2024 and now with almost 10,000 trained Emergency Dispatchers, Protocol 41 provides structured guidance that enables Emergency Dispatchers to help callers in crisis while at the same time supporting dispatcher wellness and resilience. By providing a clear, evidence-based framework for managing these complex and emotionally challenging calls, the protocol reduces uncertainty, cognitive burden, and the risk of second-guessing difficult decisions after the call has ended.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“By having this Protocol, you have given your Emergency Dispatchers a tool to alleviate somebody else’s trauma, to alleviate somebody else’s PTSD, to immediately intervene, and when they’re in their darkest place to literally give them hope, give them a way forward,” said Jonathan Flynn, the Training and Compliance Specialist for Allina Health Emergency Medical Services in St. Paul, Minnesota. “Since we implemented Protocol 41, I have yet to have a staff member come to me saying, like, hey, I had a really bad first-party mental health call, and I don&#8217;t know how to deal with it.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">As awareness of mental health challenges continues to grow nationwide, the survey findings underscore the need for consistent, specialized crisis intervention training for those who often serve as the first point of contact during an emergency. Truly a “zero-response time” solution is now available.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://prioritydispatch.widen.net/s/c8vbjm8g8c/national-suicide-month-survey-results-downloadable\">Click here</a> to download the complete survey results.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Learn more about Protocol 41: Caller in Crisis <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://get.prioritydispatch.net/protocol-41/\">here</a>.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<h4 class=\"wp-block-heading\">About the IAED:&nbsp;</h4>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch is the standard-setting organization for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and is the leading body of emergency services dispatch experts. emergencydispatch.org</p>\n\n\n\n<h4 class=\"wp-block-heading\">About Priority Dispatch: </h4>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Priority Dispatch™ is the world leader in providing research-based protocol solutions to emergency call centers in medical, fire, police, and nurse triage disciplines for over 45 years. Get to know us at prioritydispatch.net.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">&nbsp;</p>\n', 'One of 911’s Toughest Challenges: Helping Callers in Crisis', '<p>A new national survey conducted by the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch (IAED) reveals that Americans place significant trust in 911 Emergency Dispatchers during suicide-related and mental health emergencies and overwhelmingly expect them to have specialized training to handle these life-and-death calls.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', 'caller-in-crisis-survey', '', '', '2026-09-04 18:31:25', '2026-09-04 18:31:25', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2026/09/04/caller-in-crisis-survey/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1179, 6, '2026-08-24 18:13:05', '2026-08-24 18:13:05', '<h3>Proven Non-Emergency Clinical Triage Solution Will Improve Patient Access and Free Up Emergency Medical Response Resources</h3>\n<p><strong>Salt Lake City, Utah and Wales </strong>— August 24, 2026 — The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch (IAED), Priority Solutions Inc. (PSI), and Welsh Ambulance Services University NHS Trust (WAST) announced today that they have signed an intellectual property agreement for the Call Prioritisation Streaming System (CPSS), paving the way for global commercialization of the clinically proven non-emergency telephone triage system.</p>\n<p>“Modern ambulance services, internationally, respond to a broad spectrum of patient acuity, with a large percentage of 999 calls relating to conditions that are not immediately life-threatening and can be managed through alternative clinical pathways,” explains Dr. Conrad Fivaz, President, Priority Solutions Inc. “For this reason, we’re excited to bring CPSS to healthcare organizations worldwide. This proven solution we’ve built together with WAST and the IAED is going to help improve patient access and care by optimizing resources, for callers with non-life-threatening and life-threatening symptoms alike.”</p>\n<p>CPSS was developed within the 111 system and first deployed by <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://111.wales.nhs.uk/?locale=en&amp;term=A\">NHS 111 Wales</a> on April 30, 2024. Priority Solutions Inc. helped transform CPSS into a scalable solution for health systems that take urgent but non-life-threatening calls or messages directly from the public.</p>\n<p>Designed for services such as 111, 1450, nurse advice lines, and integrated urgent care, CPSS enables trained non-clinical call takers to safely prioritise patients and direct them to the most appropriate care pathway. By accurately identifying callers with potential life-threatening symptoms, the system helps reduce demand on clinical resources that are often scarce, while still ensuring patients receive timely access to appropriate care.</p>\n<p>&#8220;CPSS is built on decades of frontline non-clinical telephone triage experience and continues to evolve through evidence, research, and user feedback,” stated Dr. Mike Brady, Assistant Clinical Director for Remote Clinical Care, Welsh Ambulance Services University NHS Trust. “Health services everywhere are facing rising demand and limited clinical resources, and CPSS was designed to help meet that challenge by supporting safe, efficient non-clinical patient prioritization.”</p>\n<p>The partnership combines WAST&#8217;s decades of operational experience in telephone triage, PSI&#8217;s LowCode technology platform and Emergency Communication Nurse System (ECNS), and IAED&#8217;s internationally recognized clinical governance framework. IAED will oversee ongoing protocol governance through its established Proposals for Change process and Council of Standards, ensuring CPSS continues to evolve through evidence-based medicine, operational experience, and user feedback.</p>\n<p>CPSS is a call-handler-led process that prioritises and streams patients following initial triage. While it has traditionally been used to care for suitable lower acuity patients for alternative management pathways, its application has broadened to support the prioritisation and streaming of a wider range of patients for further assessment and decision-making within the emergency care system.</p>\n<p>CPSS is ideally suited for healthcare organizations seeking to optimize clinician capacity, improve patient navigation, and safely manage growing demand for urgent care services. For agencies already using ProQA® who are Accredited Centers of Excellence with the IAED, CPSS complements existing workflows such as PSI’s ECNS system. Organizations not using ProQA can implement CPSS with PSI’s Emergency Communication Nurse System (ECNS).</p>\n<p>###</p>\n<h4>SUPPORTING DATA:</h4>\n<p>Independently peer-reviewed research validating the system&#8217;s clinical performance includes a <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://nam10.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Finnovations.bmj.com%2Fcontent%2F11%2F2%2F89&amp;data=05%7C02%7Cwendy.whittaker%40prioritydispatch.net%7C80bdf5b78c434c81a73e08dee9e2c5a5%7C4aecbac934be4328a2ad28c8d2fc3127%7C0%7C0%7C639205357362391389%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&amp;sdata=FAu%2BbozPFlpHhNU%2FBtYKlEm%2BbDTt4hpDNfcUWRjiiIw%3D&amp;reserved=0\">2025 publication in BMJ</a> Innovations that documented the development of CPSS and reported that the platform had already managed more than 530,000 patient cases, demonstrating both its maturity and real-world operational capability. Since the 2024 go-live, CPSS has now managed over 1.6 million calls.</p>\n<p>A second <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://nam10.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fbmjopen.bmj.com%2Fcontent%2F15%2F7%2Fe097914&amp;data=05%7C02%7Cwendy.whittaker%40prioritydispatch.net%7C80bdf5b78c434c81a73e08dee9e2c5a5%7C4aecbac934be4328a2ad28c8d2fc3127%7C0%7C0%7C639205357362352221%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&amp;sdata=1C8woOzS4OcystFc3geF%2F9Vgz5PZDk1rfF4fXjIgiKc%3D&amp;reserved=0\">2025 BMJ Open evaluation</a> was performed where 83.4% of cases achieved expert clinical consensus on the appropriateness of CPSS outcomes, while clinicians agreed with those outcomes 90.5% of the time when consensus was reached. The study concluded that no significant clinical safety concerns were identified, providing confidence to patients, healthcare providers, and system stakeholders.</p>\n<h4>ABOUT PRIORITY SOLUTIONS, INC.:</h4>\n<p>Our LowCode software technology contains a base set of research-based and clinically proven protocols that have been used in more than 100,000,000 calls world-wide for over 26 years. LowCode supports ECNS and CPSS, solutions designed to provide better care to non-emergency callers while freeing up response resources and emergency lines for life-threatening calls that need the most attention. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://prioritysolutionsinc.com/\">prioritysolutionsinc.com</a></p>\n<h4>ABOUT WAST:</h4>\n<p>Welsh Ambulance Services University NHS Trust provides emergency and urgent healthcare services for people across Wales, delivering high quality and patient-led clinical care, wherever and whenever needed. WAST services include call taking, remote clinical consultation, and conveyance to an appropriate hospital or alternative treatment facility, if needed. Non-Emergency Patient Transport Service (NEPTS) takes patients to and from hospital appointments and transfers them between hospitals and treatment facilities. NHS 111 Wales provides healthcare advice 24/7 for those feeling unwell who aren&#8217;t sure where to go for help. WAST also supports Community First Responders, Co-Responders and Uniformed Responders to provide additional resources for those most in need of help in our communities. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://ambulance.nhs.wales/\">ambulance.nhs.wales</a></p>\n<h4>ABOUT THE IAED:</h4>\n<p>The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch is the standard-setting organization for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and is the leading body of emergency services dispatch experts. Our various Boards and Councils work on behalf of over 83,000 members in 60 countries—and in coordination with other influential public safety organizations—to ensure that the comprehensive system covering all aspects of emergency dispatching is safe, fast, effective, and cutting-edge. IAED protocols are used in more than 4,257 communication centers across the world in 31 languages and have been time-tested across hundreds of millions of calls for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage dispatching. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://emergencydistg.wpenginepowered.com/home\">emergencydispatch.org</a><br />&nbsp;</p>\n', 'IAED, Priority Solutions Inc., and the Welsh Ambulance Service Agree to Commercialise the Call Prioritisation Streaming System (CPSS)', '<p>The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch (IAED), Priority Solutions Inc. (PSI), and Welsh Ambulance Services University NHS Trust (WAST) announced today that they have signed an intellectual property agreement for the Call Prioritisation Streaming System (CPSS), paving the way for global commercialization of the clinically proven non-emergency telephone triage system.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', 'e5554f73-6c34-4884-afcf-7e03d9e9d497', '', '', '2026-08-24 18:13:05', '2026-08-24 18:13:05', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2026/08/24/e5554f73-6c34-4884-afcf-7e03d9e9d497/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1180, 6, '2026-06-16 19:14:50', '2026-06-16 19:14:50', '\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">New 911-Community Developed Recommended Minimum Training Guidelines for Telecommunicators Available</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><i>Consensus-Driven Update Elevates Minimum Expectations and Addresses Changes in the Industry</i></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Salt Lake City, UT – June 16, 2026 </strong>– The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch (IAED) collaborated with 911 community organizations to update the Recommended Minimum Training Guidelines for Telecommunicators. Building on the inaugural 2016 guidelines, this effort focused on better equipping 911 personnel for their expanding and evolving job functions. </p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The newly revised guidelines elevate the recommendations regarding minimum training and address changes in the 911 industry. The updated guidelines were developed by a multi-agency working group, collaborating over the last three years, to provide a consistent baseline for telecommunicator training across the United States.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The minimum training framework outlines several core categories including, Roles and Responsibilities, Legal Concepts, Interpersonal Communications, Emergency Communications Technology, Call Processing, Emergency Management, Radio Communication, Stress Management, Quality Assurance and On-The-Job Training.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">While the training is not federally mandated, it is collaboratively formulated by subject matter experts and serves as a foundation for professional development. The National Highway Traffic Safety Administration (NHTSA) National 911 Program convened the appropriate stakeholders to provide a forum for discussion. National, regional, and state 911 organizations and agencies were involved in the guideline creation, lending various voices and perspectives for inclusion. This revised guideline reflects the participants’ expertise and is supported by all participating entities.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>To view and download this document, </strong><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://prioritydispatch.widen.net/s/mztkpkvx2v/recommended-minimum-guidelines-for-initial-public-safety-telecommunicator-training--6-15--2026.\"><strong>click here</strong></a><strong>.&nbsp;<br>&nbsp;</strong></p>\n', 'Recommended Minimum Guidelines for Initial Public Safety Telecommunicator Training', '<p>This document outlines the recommended minimum training guidelines for initial Public Safety Telecommunicators (PSTs), providing a foundational framework to support entry-level 911 professionals in performing their roles effectively.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', 'minimum-guidelines-telecommunicator-training', '', '', '2026-06-16 19:14:50', '2026-06-16 19:14:50', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2026/06/16/minimum-guidelines-telecommunicator-training/', 0, 'post', '', 0);
