(1181, 6, '2026-04-27 18:13:29', '2026-04-27 18:13:29', '\n<h1 class=\"wp-block-heading\">New consortium links dispatch decisions to patient outcomes continuously</h1>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">SALT LAKE CITY, Utah, and AUSTIN, Texas, April 22, 2026 (GLOBE NEWSWIRE) – &nbsp;The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch (“IAED”) and ESO Solutions, Inc. (“ESO”), a leading data services and software provider for EMS, fire departments, hospitals, and government agencies, today announced the launch of the Network for Evidence-Based Dispatch (“NEBD”). The NEBD is a research consortium of more than a dozen EMS and fire agencies built to generate continuous, outcomes-based evidence for emergency dispatch decision-making.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The network builds on ESO and IAED’s strategic research alliance announced in November 2025 that aims to create a new era of outcomes-based research and innovation in emergency response. The NEBD—co-led by Dr. Brent Myers, chief medical officer of ESO, and Jerry Overton, president of IAED—is the next phase of this partnership.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">&#8220;Dispatchers make critical decisions without knowing what happened to the patient after the call ended,&#8221; Overton said. &#8220;The NEBD changes that, not with a single study, but with a standing group of agencies that can ask and answer questions continuously. That&#8217;s how evidence-based medicine drives stronger outcomes in the field.&#8221;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">&#8220;This consortium allows agencies to contribute to both the academic advancement of EMS protocols as well as operational enhancements to shared technologies,” Dr. Myers added. “The NEBD will shape best practices, better inform policies and foster innovations that benefit patients, providers and the EMS community at large.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The initial cohort of founding agencies represent diverse communities across the country, including large, urban EMS systems, mid-size county services and local community organizations. The consortium is designed to rapidly iterate through research questions—from alternative dispatch protocols to high-frequency chief complaints—using agency data that is already connected and ready for analysis.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">&#8220;Dispatch is where the emergency response system makes its first and often most consequential decision,&#8221; said Christopher Way, chief of Charleston County EMS and president of the National Association of EMTs. &#8220;Joining the NEBD means we have a mechanism to learn from those decisions at scale and to make sure the protocols guiding them reflect what the successful outcomes data shows us.&#8221;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">About ESO<br>ESO’s mission is to improve community health and safety outcomes through the power of data. Founded and led by emergency responders and medical professionals since 2004, ESO advances the industry by combining deep domain&nbsp;expertise&nbsp;with innovative technology, impactful&nbsp;research&nbsp;and the industry’s largest integrated emergency outcome data asset. The company delivers the world&#8217;s most trusted and connected emergency ecosystem—an open, interoperable platform that unites emergency medical response, fire,&nbsp;hospital&nbsp;and government stakeholders across the full emergency continuum through real-time data exchange and embedded intelligence in frontline workflows. ESO’s solutions deliver actionable insights to decision-makers, enable smarter coordination across the emergency continuum and uphold&nbsp;the highest standards of data security and patient privacy. The company helps customers around the world deliver measurable improvements in clinical,&nbsp;operational&nbsp;and financial outcomes with dedicated teams in the United States, Canada, United Kingdom, Denmark, Czech&nbsp;Republic, India&nbsp;and Costa Rica. For more information, visit&nbsp;www.eso.com.&nbsp;</p>\n', 'IAED and ESO Launch the Network for Evidence-Based Dispatch', '<p>The NEBD is a research consortium of more than a dozen EMS and fire agencies built to generate continuous, outcomes-based evidence for emergency dispatch decision-making.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', 'iaed-eso-launch-network-for-evidence-based-dispatch', '', '', '2026-04-27 18:13:29', '2026-04-27 18:13:29', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2026/04/27/iaed-eso-launch-network-for-evidence-based-dispatch/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1182, 6, '2026-04-24 19:47:08', '2026-04-24 19:47:08', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Media contact: Pam Stewart, 385-429-9391, <a href=\"mailto:pam.stewart@emergencydispatch.org\">pam.stewart@emergencydispatch.org</a>&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>April 24, 2026 (Las Vegas, NV)</strong></p><p>— Jan Althoff from North Memorial Health Ambulance in Minnesota is receiving top honors today from the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAED™), the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and the leading body of emergency dispatch experts.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The Dr. Jeff J. Clawson Leadership Award is given annually to an individual or group of individuals making significant leadership contributions to the emergency dispatch profession. The award is being announced at IAED’s 2026 <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://navigator.emergencydispatch.org/home\">NAVIGATOR </a>Conference in Las Vegas, which is bringing together more than 1,600 emergency services personnel from across the country and the world to stay abreast of best practices in emergency dispatch protocols.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Jan started her journey in Emergency Medical Dispatch in 1991. She then went on to become an ED-Q in 2001, was a driving force for North Memorial Health Ambulance achieving IAED ACE accreditation in 2004, and continues her work in Quality Assurance and Improvement to this day.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">On top of her work as an Emergency Dispatcher, Jan is being recognized for her invaluable work on the Medical Council of Standards subcommittee tasked with revamping Protocol 33: Transfer/Interfacility/Palliative Care and creating Protocol 37: Interfacility Evaluation/Transfer for the Medical Priority Dispatch System™. She has also used her years of expertise in emergency medical ground and air transport to play an essential role in creating the Medical Protocol Transfer Suite, which helps Emergency Dispatchers offer safe, efficient, and cost-effective patient transfers between facilities. The Medical Transfer Protocols are Protocol 45: Specialized Unscheduled Up-Care Transports, Protocol 46: Scheduled Interfacility Transfers (Routine), and Protocol 47: Mental Health Transfers.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The award is named for IAED’s founder, Jeff Clawson, MD, who developed the standardized emergency response protocols used by emergency dispatch centers around the world.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“For some 35 years now, Jan’s leadership in the field of Emergency Dispatch has fostered the improvement of protocol performance, collaboration, and, ultimately, patient care. We at the Academy owe Jan a sincere debt of gratitude,” said Dr. Clawson.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A huge congratulations to Jan for her extraordinary work in the Emergency Dispatch realm and on receiving this award of significant achievement and excellence.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For more information about IAED, visit <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"http://www.emergencydispatch.org/\">www.emergencydispatch.org</a>.</p>\n\n\n\n<h4 class=\"wp-block-heading\">About the IAED</h4>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The IAED is the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and is the leading body of emergency dispatch experts. Our various boards and councils work on behalf of over 80,000 members in 60 countries—and in coordination with other influential public safety organizations—to ensure that the comprehensive system of emergency dispatching is safe, fast, effective and cutting-edge. IAED protocols are used in more than 4,216 communications centers across the world and have been time-tested across hundreds of millions of calls for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage dispatching. Learn more at <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"http://www.emergencydispatch.org/\">EmergencyDispatch.org</a>.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">###</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">&nbsp;</p>\n', 'Medical Transfer Protocol Specialist, Jan Althoff, Receives Top Honors from the IAED’s Dr. Clawson', '<p>Jan Althoff from North Memorial Health Ambulance in Minnesota is receiving top honors today from the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAED™), the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and the leading body of emergency dispatch experts.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', '5811d08e-1531-4171-bd87-85ad19b5cc8e', '', '', '2026-04-24 19:47:08', '2026-04-24 19:47:08', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2026/04/24/5811d08e-1531-4171-bd87-85ad19b5cc8e/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1183, 6, '2026-04-23 01:43:27', '2026-04-23 01:43:27', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Media contact: Matthew Miko (801) 245-0579; <a href=\"mailto:matthew.miko@emergencydispatch.org\">matthew.miko@emergencydispatch.org</a></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>April 22, 2026 (Las Vegas, NV)</strong></p><p>— The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAEDTM), the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch services worldwide, today announced that Las Vegas Fire &amp; Rescue’s Combined Communications Center has earned the IAED’s Accredited Center of Excellence (ACE) designation for emergency fire dispatch.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The IAED’s ACE credential is the highest distinction awarded to emergency communication centers, certifying that an agency is performing at or above internationally recognized standards for best practices in emergency response. To achieve accreditation, agencies must successfully complete a rigorous review of 20 Points of Accreditation, including strong local governance, comprehensive quality improvement processes, and a demonstrated commitment to continuous, data-driven improvement.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“The Combined Communications Center staff demonstrated dedication and teamwork in achieving accreditation. Their commitment ensured all standards were met and opportunities for improvement were addressed,” said Melanie L. Straeter, Communications Quality Improvement Coordinator with Las Vegas Fire &amp; Rescue. “The professional and technical growth of Fire Communications members has resulted in high-quality service on every call. We are proud of our team and the enhanced service this brings to Las Vegas residents and visitors.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Alongside Las Vegas Fire &amp; Rescue, 73 other emergency dispatch centers representing medical, fire, police and emergency nurse triage responders worldwide have earned or re-earned the IAED’s Accredited Center of Excellence (ACE) designation. They will all be honored in the opening session of the NAVIGATOR conference in Las Vegas, NV this week.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Receiving this honor while NAVIGATOR is being held in their backyard, makes this year extra special for Las Vegas Fire &amp; Rescue.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“ACE Accreditation reflects our Combined Communications Center’s commitment to continuous growth,” said Fernando Gray, Fire Chief. “While many practices were already in place, this process reinforced service quality standards, the importance of fidelity in service delivery, and led to improved outcomes. This designation proves what I have always felt: our LVFR Fire Communications Team is one of the best in the world. Congratulations, Team.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Less than 10% of all emergency communication centers worldwide are ACE accredited, a distinction that is valid for a three-year period, during which all standards must be continually upheld. Emergency communication centers may earn multiple accreditations, one for each discipline they serve, including fire, medical, police, and emergency nurse triage. The Priority Dispatch System™ is recognized and used in more than 60 countries worldwide as the highest standard of care for emergency dispatch.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Accreditation is truly a pinnacle achievement,” said Christof Chwojka, Chair of the IAED Accreditation Board. “We commend the calltakers, dispatchers, and leadership at Las Vegas Fire &amp; Rescue for their dedication to excellence and adherence to the highest standards. The ACE credential assures their community that their first responders are supported by world-class emergency communication.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://prioritydispatch.widen.net/s/dnwrz8vtdb/ace-nav-recognition-2026\">&gt;&gt; To view the entire list of recipients by state, click here.</a></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For more information about IAED accreditation, visit <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://emergencydistg.wpenginepowered.com/what-we-do/accreditation\">https://emergencydistg.wpenginepowered.com/what-we-do/accreditation</a>.</p>\n\n\n\n<h4 class=\"wp-block-heading\">About the IAED</h4>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The IAED is the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and is the leading body of emergency dispatch experts. Our various boards and councils work on behalf of over 80,000 members in 60 countries—and in coordination with other influential public safety organizations—to ensure that the comprehensive system of emergency dispatching is safe, fast, effective and cutting-edge. IAED protocols are used in more than 4,216 communications centers across the world and have been time-tested across hundreds of millions of calls for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage dispatching. Learn more at <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"http://www.emergencydispatch.org/\">EmergencyDispatch.org</a></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">###<br>&nbsp;</p>\n', 'Las Vegas Fire &#038; Rescue Earns “Center of Excellence” Honor from the IAED', '<p>The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAEDTM), the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch services worldwide, today announced that Las Vegas Fire &#038; Rescue’s Combined Communications Center has earned the IAED’s Accredited Center of Excellence (ACE) designation for emergency fire dispatch.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', '9e84b643-bc95-445b-80f8-45786a11c52c', '', '', '2026-04-23 01:43:27', '2026-04-23 01:43:27', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2026/04/23/9e84b643-bc95-445b-80f8-45786a11c52c/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1184, 6, '2026-04-23 01:36:44', '2026-04-23 01:36:44', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Media contact: Pam Stewart, 385-429-9391, <a href=\"mailto:pam.stewart@emergencydispatch.org\">pam.stewart@emergencydispatch.org</a>&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>April 17, 2025 (ORLANDO, FL) </strong>— The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch’s® Emergency Telecommunicator (ETC) Course celebrates its 25th anniversary this week with a local panel of ETC students at their annual NAVIGATOR Conference held in Las Vegas.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In addition to professional development sessions and networking at NAVIGATOR, more than 1,600 emergency response professionals will have the chance to learn about the ETC program in multiple sessions and hear from a panel of current and prior ETC students from the Veterans Tribute Career &amp; Technical Academy, a Las Vegas high school focused on preparing students for careers in public service.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Emergency dispatch veteran, Liz Satterlee has been the ETC Course Instructor at the Veterans Tribute Career &amp; Technical Academy since 2023 and knows first-hand how important the ETC curriculum is to begin training the next generation of Emergency Dispatchers. She teaches 30 to 60 students each year, including some of those on this year’s panel, and has seen many of them go on to become Emergency Dispatchers and work in other related fields.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“I love that it’s given me the structure and I rely on it a lot,” Satterlee said about the ETC course. “We have the lab to practice the calls, but without having that manual, that guide, and the ETC Certification, I don’t know how far I would have gotten in progressing students their senior year.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Born out of the desire to fill more emergency dispatch center vacancies with truly dedicated and service-minded individuals, the Emergency Telecommunicator Course builds a pipeline for recruitment while supporting earlier outreach to high school students. Participants are given introductions to customer service, call management, structured calltaking, advanced technology (GPS, CAD), call routing, radio use, disaster readiness, civics, and legal aspects of emergency dispatch.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“The Emergency Telecommunicator Course offers a comprehensive foundation for individuals seeking a career in 911 communications, as well as those pursuing professions across the broader public safety field. The curriculum provides valuable insight into becoming a dispatcher and highlights the ongoing skills, dedication, and commitment required throughout one’s career,” said ETC Program Coordinator, Bonni Stockman.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">After 20 years as an Emergency Dispatcher and 3.5 years teaching ETC, Liz Satterlee knows that being an Emergency Dispatcher shapes your life, even when you aren’t on duty—and that’s why the ETC course is so important. For the past 25 years, ETC has been helping emergency communications centers and other businesses around the United States find and draw in new generations of Emergency Dispatchers that the public can rely on.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“Developed by professionals with years of firsthand experience, the course brings the role to life through their passion, expertise, and deep respect for the profession,” Stockman said. “I have truly enjoyed working with the ETC program and helping introduce this meaningful career to the next generation. Their enthusiasm, fresh perspective, and strong connection to technology bring an exciting energy that will continue to strengthen and advance the field.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For more information about IAED, visit <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"http://www.emergencydispatch.org/\">www.emergencydispatch.org</a>.</p>\n\n\n\n<h4 class=\"wp-block-heading\">About the IAED</h4>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The IAED is the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and is the leading body of emergency dispatch experts. Our various boards and councils work on behalf of over 80,000 members in 60 countries—and in coordination with other influential public safety organizations—to ensure that the comprehensive system of emergency dispatching is safe, fast, effective and cutting-edge. IAED protocols are used in more than 4,216 communications centers across the world and have been time-tested across hundreds of millions of calls for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage dispatching. Learn more at <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"http://www.emergencydispatch.org/\">EmergencyDispatch.org</a>.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">###</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">&nbsp;</p>\n', 'The IAED’s ETC Course Celebrates 25 Years with Las Vegas High School Student Panel', '<p>The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch’s® Emergency Telecommunicator (ETC) Course celebrates its 25th anniversary this week with a local panel of ETC students at their annual NAVIGATOR Conference held in Las Vegas.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', 'cbda1e66-d3d3-45f9-9213-c0a2c7d8a371', '', '', '2026-04-23 01:36:44', '2026-04-23 01:36:44', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2026/04/23/cbda1e66-d3d3-45f9-9213-c0a2c7d8a371/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1185, 6, '2026-04-23 01:36:31', '2026-04-23 01:36:31', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Media contact: Pam Stewart, 385-429-9391, <a href=\"mailto:pam.stewart@emergencydispatch.org\">pam.stewart@emergencydispatch.org</a></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><i>Given by the IAED, these awards honor a 911 instructor and dispatcher separately for excellence in their roles this year</i></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>April 22, 2026 (Las Vegas, NV)</strong></p><p>— Today, the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAEDTM), the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and the leading body of emergency dispatch experts, honored two recipients for their above and beyond excellence in service. Chris Clark of Baker City, OR received the 2026 Dave Massengale Memorial Instructor of the Year Award while Nathan Estes of Ocala, FL, received the Dispatcher of the Year Award. &nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">These awards are given annually to a North American emergency dispatch instructor and Emergency Dispatcher who exemplify excellence in his or her work throughout the year and were announced this morning at the opening session of the IAED’s 2026 <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://navigator.emergencydispatch.org/home\">NAVIGATOR </a>Conference in Las Vegas. The annual conference brings together more than 1,600 emergency services leaders from across the country and the world to stay abreast of the best practices in emergency dispatch protocols.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Chris has been an EFD instructor with the IAED since 2016 and has taught over 326 courses! Outside of Chris’ many hours spent teaching, he is an incredible mentor and trainer and spends lots of time team teaching with new instructors and helping them become as effective as he is. His already widespread influence reaches even further as he also volunteers on the Fire curriculum board and has been instrumental in the rollout of updating courses to the most current version, Fire v8.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“Chris consistently receives very very positive reviews on all the courses he teaches, he is out there a ton, he affects a lot of students, and the reach of his instruction goes incredibly far. Not just through the courses he teaches, but through all of the instructors that he mentors and the curriculum he affects. If you start adding up all the people that he has touched, we are probably in the millions,” said Eric Fayad, the Associate Director of Instructor Services with the IAED.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Nathan has been with Marion County Public Safety Communications in Ocala, Florida for seven years. He is certified in EMD, EFD, and EPD and is consistently highly compliant in all three discipline protocols. Nathan is the first ever Dispatcher of the Year award recipient for a call using the Emergency Police Protocols and demonstrated extreme compassion, calm, and competence on a call with a 13-year-old girl lost in the forest.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“When I heard the call, the first thing I noticed was how compassionate and kind Nathan was. You could just hear the scared girl calm right down almost immediately,” said Pam Stewart, IAED Executive Director of Operations and Board of Certification Chair. “He has this massive compassion, and it was just a great call, but this award is never just about the call that is submitted, it’s about the totality of what the nominee does for their agency, their community, and the general public. As one of the IAED’s most prestigious award recipients, we extend a heartfelt congratulations to Nathan and thank everyone who submitted nominations this year.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">As a non-profit with worldwide reach, the IAED provides training, education, and certification for more than 80,000 emergency dispatchers in 60 countries. The Dave Massengale Memorial Instructor of the Year Award and the Dispatcher of the Year Award are two of the most distinctive awards presented by the IAED. The Instructor of the Year award was created to identify and celebrate individuals who have made the most significant contributions throughout the year to further the education and training of other professionals in the field. The Dispatcher of the Year award was created to identify and celebrate individuals who have made the most significant contributions throughout the year to further the values of IAED and emergency dispatch excellence.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“Emergency dispatchers are the first first responders, the voices we hear in our worst moments when help is needed,” said Stewart. “These awards recognize instructors and dispatchers who go above and beyond to answer the call of duty and exemplify excellence under pressure.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The IAED also annually honors Dispatchers of the Year in the U.K. &amp; Ireland, Europe, Asia, the Middle East, China, and Australasia.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For more information about the IAED, visit <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"http://www.emergencydispatch.org/\">www.emergencydispatch.org</a>.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">About the IAED</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The IAED is the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and is the leading body of emergency dispatch experts. Our various boards and councils work on behalf of over 80,000 members in 60 countries—and in coordination with other influential public safety organizations—to ensure that the comprehensive system of emergency dispatching is safe, fast, effective, and cutting-edge. IAED protocols are used in more than 4,216 communications centers across the world and have been time-tested across hundreds of millions of calls for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage dispatching. Learn more at <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"http://www.emergencydispatch.org/\">EmergencyDispatch.org</a>.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">###</p>\n', 'Nathan Estes and Chris Clark Receive Top Honors as North America’s 911 Dispatcher and Instructor of the Year', '<p>Today, the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAEDTM), the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and the leading body of emergency dispatch experts, honored two recipients for their above and beyond excellence in service. Chris Clark of Baker City, OR received the 2026 Dave Massengale Memorial Instructor of the Year Award while Nathan Est</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', '8e8f948b-cfe4-440f-a805-bdb78a35f524', '', '', '2026-04-23 01:36:31', '2026-04-23 01:36:31', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2026/04/23/8e8f948b-cfe4-440f-a805-bdb78a35f524/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1186, 6, '2026-03-09 20:07:39', '2026-03-09 20:07:39', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Salt Lake City, UT — March 10, 2026</strong>—One‑year analysis shows a 21% improvement in response times for the most critical calls and a signification reduction in overtime for the Georgian Central Ambulance Communications Centre after implementing the Medical Priority Dispatch System.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">&#8220;The intent by the province with this more sophisticated dispatch system is to get paramedics to calls — urgent, life-threatening calls — more quickly. We are seeing exactly that,&#8221; said Jane Sinclair, the county’s general manager of health and emergency services.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch (IAED) congratulates CACC on these significant improvements achieved in their first full year using the <strong>Medical Priority Dispatch System (MPDS)</strong>. According to a one‑year analysis presented to Simcoe County Council, MPDS has <i>“fundamentally changed”</i> how call priorities are distributed and has contributed to major operational enhancements across the service.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">During the review period, overall call volume remained stable; however, CACC reported markedly better call‑volume management and a healthier work environment. Officials attribute these improvements to MPDS’s precision in triage, noting:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>More accurate prioritization</strong>,</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>More appropriate resource allocation</strong>, and a</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Significant reduction in overtime</strong>, allowing for more consistent rest periods and more effective scheduling.</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Jane Sinclair further stated that staff were <i>very pleased to see the result of this new system,”</i> noting that MPDS&#8217;s structured question‑and‑answer process has also increased accuracy in determining call severity, reducing the chances of mis‑triage. As Sinclair emphasized, <i>“MPDS… ask[s] more questions, so the level of accuracy is much better.”</i></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Frontline performance data further reflects the system’s impact:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Paramedics arrived first on scene for urgent/emergency calls <strong>41% of the time</strong>, up from 34%.</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Fire services were dispatched to <strong>76% of sudden cardiac arrests</strong>, up from 72%.</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Combined fire–paramedic arrival within six minutes for sudden cardiac arrest improved to <strong>59%</strong>, up from 55%.</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">&#8220;Georgian Central Ambulance Communications Centre&#8217;s results are exactly what the MPDS is designed to deliver: faster care for the sickest patients, improved responder readiness, and clearer decision‑making at every stage of the call,” said Dr. Jeff Clawson, Medical Director and Founder of the IAED. “Their first‑year outcomes affirm the global standard that structured, protocol‑based dispatching saves time, reduces risk, and ultimately saves lives.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Local leaders have responded positively. Wasaga Beach Mayor Brian Smith stated he initially had concerns but now recognizes the change as a <i>“massive improvement.”&nbsp;</i>“This is essential to life saving in our communities,” Smith added.</p>\n', 'IAED Applauds 21% Improvement in Response Times for Life-Threatening Calls for Georgian Central Ambulance Communications Centre', '<p>One year analysis shows a 21% improvement in response times for the most critical calls and a signification reduction in overtime for Georgian Central Ambulance Communications Centre in Ontario, Canada, after implementing the Medical Priority Dispatch System.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', '8b2b55ef-c348-4580-bdf7-0571d564d809', '', '', '2026-03-09 20:07:39', '2026-03-09 20:07:39', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2026/03/09/8b2b55ef-c348-4580-bdf7-0571d564d809/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1187, 6, '2025-11-04 13:59:26', '2025-11-04 13:59:26', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><i>The global alliance links dispatch decisions to patient outcomes, advancing emergency response and unlocking innovation</i></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Austin, Texas and Salt Lake City, Utah (Nov. 4, 2025) </strong>— <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.eso.com/\">ESO Solutions, Inc.</a> (“ESO”), a leading data services and software provider for EMS, fire departments, hospitals, and government agencies, and <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://emergencydistg.wpenginepowered.com/home\">The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAED)</a>, the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch and response services worldwide, have formed a strategic research alliance to advance outcomes-based emergency dispatch worldwide for the first time.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">This partnership brings together ESO’s robust, outcomes-linked data ecosystem and IAED’s globally trusted dispatch protocols to create a new era of outcomes-based research and innovation in emergency response. In collaboration, IAED and ESO will demonstrate the impact of dispatch decisions in time-sensitive emergencies and accelerate the evidence-base for alternative response, needs-matched resourcing, and affordable, sustainable healthcare delivery.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“The collaboration between IAED and ESO is a powerful step forward for the global emergency response community,” said Jerry Overton, President of IAED. “By leveraging integrated data and rigorous research, we can address the challenges facing emergency communications centers, emergency medical services, ambulances services and fire departments—rising call volumes, resource strain, and the need for sustainable solutions. Together, we will set new standards for evidence-based dispatch and empower smarter, safer responses.”<br>By validating dispatch protocols with real-world outcomes, the alliance will empower organizations to allocate and deploy resources more effectively—unlocking transformational value from emergency dispatch and evidenced-based response.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Together, ESO and IAED will deliver joint research projects, offer co-hosted research development workshops, and support peer-reviewed publications that validate dispatch protocols and inform system design using outcomes data. ESO’s integrated data will enable granular, protocol-level analyses and ongoing prospective research to define best practices, develop outcomes-based system status management, and support healthcare policy for improved and sustainable delivery. This approach addresses specific community needs, strengthens global standards, and drives innovation in dispatch and emergency response globally.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“At ESO, we believe that outcome-driven insights are the key to advancing emergency response. Partnering with IAED allows us to link dispatch, ambulance, fire and hospital outcomes in a way that has never been done before,” said Eric Beck, President &amp; CEO of ESO. “This partnership will not only quantify the impact of dispatch decisions in time-sensitive emergencies, but also drive innovation in response models, system design and system status management—ultimately improving outcomes for patients, responders and communities worldwide.”</p>\n\n\n\n<h4 class=\"wp-block-heading\">About The IAED</h4>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch is the standard-setting organization for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and is the leading body of emergency services dispatch experts. Our various Boards and Councils work on behalf of over 83,000 members in 60 countries—and in coordination with other influential public safety organizations—to ensure that the comprehensive system covering all aspects of emergency dispatching is safe, fast, effective, and cutting-edge. IAED protocols are used in more than 4,257 communication centers across the world in 31 languages and have been time-tested across hundreds of millions of calls for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage dispatching. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://emergencydispatch.org/\">emergencydispatch.org</a></p>\n\n\n\n<h4 class=\"wp-block-heading\">About ESO</h4>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"http://www.eso.com/\">ESO</a>’s mission is to improve community health and safety outcomes through the power of data. Founded by emergency responders and medical professionals in 2004, ESO advances the industry by combining deep domain expertise with innovative technology, impactful research and the industry’s largest emergency data asset. The company delivers the world&#8217;s most trusted and connected emergency ecosystem—an open, interoperable platform that unites emergency medical response, fire, hospital and government stakeholders across the full emergency continuum. The ecosystem is powered by rich data, embedded intelligence and purpose-built solutions that help emergency care teams deliver better outcomes. ESO’s solutions are designed to support intelligent workflows and provide insights to decision-makers all while maintaining the highest standards of data security and patient privacy. The company helps customers around the world deliver measurable improvements in clinical, operational and financial outcomes with dedicated teams in the United States, Canada, United Kingdom, Denmark, Czech Republic, and Costa Rica. For more information, visit <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"http://www.eso.com/\">www.eso.com</a>.</p>\n\n\n\n<h4 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Media Contacts:</h4>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For ESO,&nbsp;<br>Hope Sander<br>Red Fan Communications<br><a href=\"mailto:eso@redfancommunications.com\">eso@redfancommunications.com</a>&nbsp;<br>737-280-8783</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For IAED,<br>Pam Stewart<br>Senior Director of Operations<br><a href=\"mailto:pam.stewart@emergencydispatch.org\">pam.stewart@emergencydispatch.org</a> &nbsp;<br>385-429-9391<br>&nbsp;</p>\n', 'ESO and IAED Form Strategic Partnership to Advance Evidence-Based  Emergency Dispatch and Response', '<p>ESO Solutions, Inc. (“ESO”), a leading data services and software provider for EMS, fire departments, hospitals, and government agencies, and The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAED), the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch and response services worldwide, have formed a strategic research alliance to advance outcomes-based emergency dispatch worldwide for the firs</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', 'eso-partnership', '', '', '2025-11-04 13:59:26', '2025-11-04 13:59:26', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2025/11/04/eso-partnership/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1188, 6, '2025-09-15 20:44:18', '2025-09-15 20:44:18', '<h2>Great Lakes Region Leads the Way in Equipping 911 Emergency Dispatchers with Training and Tools for Suicidal Callers<br />&nbsp;</h2>\n<p>Media Contact:<br />Darren Judd, 385-429-9393<br /><a href=\"mailto:darren.judd@emergencydispatch.org\">darren.judd@emergencydispatch.org</a></p>\n<p>Salt Lake City, Utah, September 15, 2025 – For Suicide Prevention Month, the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAEDTM) recognizes all Emergency Dispatchers who play such a vital role in mental health crisis response every day. In 2023 alone, 911 Emergency Communications Centers (ECCs) took an estimated 24 million mental health crisis calls, compared to the 16 million calls, texts, and chats received by 988 since its launch in 2022. (Source: <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.wwlp.com/news/massachusetts/states-still-wrestling-with-crisis-call-response-solutions/\">WWLP – States still wrestling with crisis call response solutions</a>)&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Ty Wooten, Director of Governmental Affairs with the IAED, said, “The majority of people in a mental health crisis still call 911, even three years after the launch of the 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline.” A recent survey of Emergency Dispatchers by the IAED found that 40% felt they did not have adequate tools or guidance to manage first-party callers with suicide risk or ideation. Wooten continued, “Emergency Dispatchers need tools and training to handle these calls with confidence in order to create optimal outcomes for these at-risk callers.”&nbsp;</p>\n<p>To address this need, three ECCs in the Great Lakes Region adopted a new suicide risk and ideation calltaking tool and training in 2024. In Illinois, Brandon Miller, Executive Director with Illinois Valley Regional Dispatch (IVRD), agreed that Emergency Dispatchers haven’t historically been equipped for these calls, and that when he was an Emergency Dispatcher he didn’t feel confident about what to say to callers who were in crisis or suicidal. “There were a lot of myths out there about what kind of things you should or shouldn’t say,” Miller said, and continued, “We know the toll that mental health crisis calls take on our Emergency Dispatchers, even potentially affecting their careers.”&nbsp;</p>\n<p>This is why when the IAED released Protocol 41: Caller in Crisis (1st Party Only) one year ago, Miller says his agency implemented it as quickly as they could. Protocol 41 is a first-of-its-kind tool for 911 with specialized training for suicide risk and ideation callers. The Protocol has an Emotional Control Tool with 20 different emotional states to help connect with and direct these fragile callers while first responders are on the way. Miller says even if other ECCs have no plans to transfer calls to 988 or change their responses for these calls, “there’s extreme value for Emergency Dispatchers to have Protocol 41 and its Emotional Control Tool in their toolbox in order to help people in their community calling in that might be suicidal or having a major crisis in their lives.”</p>\n<p>IVRD has begun transferring some calls to 988, in compliance with an Illinois mandate called the Community Emergency Services and Support Act requiring 911 centers to coordinate with 988. Miller’s center became a pilot for the state, along with about a dozen others, by being a first adopter of Protocol 41: Caller in Crisis (1st Party Only). When talking with a caller in crisis, the Protocol guides his Emergency Dispatchers through triage to dispatch the appropriate response—or now transfer to 988. Miller said, “It&#8217;s not just about transferring calls off. It&#8217;s about giving your Emergency Dispatchers the tools and the ability for them to talk people through a crisis until the responders arrive.”</p>\n<p>In Plainfield, Indiana, Hendricks County Communications Center has also been an early adopter of Protocol 41. Meghan Monaco, Quality Assurance Manager, spoke about how the Protocol has been working well for their Emergency Dispatchers: “We can stay on the line and discuss more meaningful topics. There is more fluidity in how we address the patient and reassure them that we are listening to what they are saying. We have more tools to address each potential emotional state the caller could be experiencing. We have three confirmed saves due to the use of Protocol 41. Our law enforcement partners have also expressed praise, including a commendation for an Emergency Dispatcher and a save that was a direct result of Protocol 41.”</p>\n<p>In St. Paul, Minnesota, Jonathan Flynn is the Training and Compliance Specialist for Allina Health Emergency Medical Services, the largest EMS provider in the state. Allina Health implemented Protocol 41 in November 2024. Flynn talked about how calls with a suicidal person are different from other 911 calls like those involving cardiac arrest, where something has already happened to the patient. “With a caller in a mental health crisis, Emergency Dispatchers have an immediate impact and the immediate ability to impact that call—this is as close as they get to actually treating a patient.” Flynn said Allina Health’s implementation of Protocol 41 has been overwhelmingly positive. “By having this Protocol, you have given your telecommunicators a tool to alleviate somebody else’s trauma, to alleviate somebody else’s PTSD, to immediately intervene, and when they’re in their darkest place to literally give them hope, give them a way forward.”</p>\n<p>So far, several hundred agencies and several thousand Emergency Dispatchers have completed this essential protocol and training. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://nam10.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fget.prioritydispatch.net%2Fprotocol-41%2F&amp;data=05%7C02%7Cwendy.whittaker%40prioritydispatch.net%7C590719a054b647f91e4608ddf0a2df57%7C4aecbac934be4328a2ad28c8d2fc3127%7C0%7C0%7C638931304504114645%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&amp;sdata=4rsaLtzW395H%2Fy6TY5Nt4a3oG5neSImB5IOV6OtSISA%3D&amp;reserved=0\">More information on Protocol 41</a></p>\n<h4>About the IAED:</h4>\n<p>The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch is the standard-setting organization for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and is the leading body of emergency services dispatch experts. Our various Boards and Councils work on behalf of over 83,000 members in 60 countries—and in coordination with other influential public safety organizations—to ensure that the comprehensive system covering all aspects of emergency dispatching is safe, fast, effective, and cutting-edge. IAED protocols are used in more than 4,257 communication centers across the world in 31 languages and have been time-tested across hundreds of millions of calls for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage dispatching. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://emergencydispatch.org\">emergencydispatch.org</a><br />&nbsp;</p>\n', 'Most Mental Crisis Calls Still Answered by 911, Not 988', '<p>During National Suicide Prevention Month, the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAEDTM) recognizes all Emergency Dispatchers who play such a vital role in mental health crisis response every day.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', 'a54fb8b5-1288-45df-bd1a-8c36f0bc4e35', '', '', '2025-09-15 20:44:18', '2025-09-15 20:44:18', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2025/09/15/a54fb8b5-1288-45df-bd1a-8c36f0bc4e35/', 0, 'post', '', 0);
