(1189, 6, '2025-04-17 17:28:58', '2025-04-17 17:28:58', '\n<h4 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Media contact</h4>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Pam Stewart<br><a href=\"tel:385-429-9391\" data-type=\"tel\" data-id=\"tel:385-429-9391\">385-429-9391<br></a><a href=\"mailto:pam.stewart@emergencydispatch.org\">pam.stewart@emergencydispatch.org</a> </p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><i><strong>April 17, 2025 (ORLANDO, FL)</strong></i> — The Emergency Dispatch Learning Portal Team located in Salt Lake City, UT is receiving top honors today from the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAED), the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and the leading body of emergency dispatch experts.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The Dr. Jeff J. Clawson Leadership Award is given annually to an individual or group of individuals making significant leadership contributions to the emergency dispatch profession. The award is being announced at IAED’s 2025 <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://navigator.emergencydispatch.org/home\">NAVIGATOR </a>Conference in Orlando, which is bringing together more than 1,500 emergency services personnel from across the country and the world to stay abreast of best practices in emergency dispatch protocols.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The Emergency Dispatch Learning Portal Team consists of six members responsible for creating an updated learning portal to enhance the training and certification process for over 70,000 Emergency Dispatchers worldwide. This innovative platform is designed to ensure that Emergency Dispatchers have ready access to courses that help them upskill year over year, while providing a smooth process for emergency call center leaders and training supervisors to purchase customized training plans. The team is led by Andrew Palmer, the Associate Director of Learning and Software Development; Jared Stillman, Software Developer Manager; Hien Cam, Software Developer II; Drew Woodmansee, Software Developer II; Kameron (Tate) Player, Software Developer II; and Nathan Novak, Database Administrator.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">&#8220;We’re excited to introduce a platform that more fully addresses the needs of our users,&#8221; said Matt Miko, Director of Academics, Research, and Communications for the IAED. &#8220;The tens of thousands of courses that are taken yearly mean our learning portal needs to make it easy to onboard, train, and keep Emergency Dispatchers certified. This ensures that our certified Emergency Dispatchers are always ready to provide the highest level of service to the public.&#8221;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The award is named for IAED’s founder, Jeff Clawson, MD, who developed the standardized emergency response protocols used by emergency dispatch centers around the world.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“This is the first time we’ve given an award to such a recent event, but it has been so groundbreaking, so earth shaking, at least for me and folks that run the academy, that it definitely warranted this recognition, and I can’t be any prouder of these people,” said Dr. Clawson.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Congratulations to the Emergency Dispatch Learning Portal Team on receiving this award of significant achievement and excellence.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For more information about IAED, visit <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"http://www.emergencydispatch.org/\">www.emergencydispatch.org.</a></p>\n\n\n\n<h4 class=\"wp-block-heading\">About the IAED</h4>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The IAED is the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and is the leading body of emergency dispatch experts. Our various boards and councils work on behalf of over 80,000 members in 60 countries—and in coordination with other influential public safety organizations—to ensure that the comprehensive system of emergency dispatching is safe, fast, effective and cutting-edge. IAED protocols are used in more than 4,216 communications centers across the world and have been time-tested across hundreds of millions of calls for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage dispatching. Learn more at <a href=\"http://www.emergencydispatch.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">EmergencyDispatch.org</a>.<br> </p>\n', 'Emergency Dispatch Learning Portal Team Receives Top Honors from the IAED’s Dr. Clawson', '<p>The Emergency Dispatch Learning Portal Team located in Salt Lake City, UT is receiving top honors today from the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAED), the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and the leading body of emergency dispatch experts.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', '7ad5e310-b8c5-491a-b9fe-58b4e805fdd8', '', '', '2025-04-17 17:28:58', '2025-04-17 17:28:58', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2025/04/17/7ad5e310-b8c5-491a-b9fe-58b4e805fdd8/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1190, 6, '2025-04-15 22:50:34', '2025-04-15 22:50:34', '\n<h4 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Media contact:&nbsp;</h4>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Pam Stewart<br>385-429-9391<br><a href=\"mailto:pam.stewart@emergencydispatch.org\">pam.stewart@emergencydispatch.org</a></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><i><strong>April 15, 2025 (Orlando, FL) </strong></i>— Today, the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAEDTM), the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and the leading body of emergency dispatch experts, honored two recipients for their above and beyond excellence in service. Jody Mader of Butler County, KS, received the 2025 Dave Massengale Memorial Instructor of the Year Award while Danielle King of Syosset, NY, received the 911 Dispatcher of the Year Award.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">These awards are given annually to a North American emergency dispatch instructor and Emergency Dispatcher who exemplify excellence in his or her work throughout the year and were announced this morning at the opening session of the IAED’s 2025 <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://navigator.emergencydispatch.org/home\">NAVIGATOR</a> Conference in Orlando. The annual conference brings together more than 1,500 emergency services leaders from across the country and the world to stay abreast of the best practices in emergency dispatch protocols.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Jody has been an ETC and Triple Q instructor since 2015, has taught over 218 courses, and is solely responsible for teaching 30 percent of the county’s total ETC instructors in that time. She also often works the night shift at her agency, doing double duty and providing exceptional instruction and service day in and day out.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Danielle is a proficient, dedicated, and compassionate Emergency Dispatcher and volunteer EMT with her local fire department. “Danielle’s proficiency is evident in her rapid and accurate triage of emergency calls,” said James De Bono, manager of Northwell Health Emergency Medical Services where Danielle works. “She obtains critical information and assesses situations quickly, ensuring the appropriate resources are sent to patients in need. Her calm and focused demeanor under pressure ensures her callers and patients receive the assistance they need.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">As a non-profit with worldwide reach, the IAED provides training, education, and certification for more than 80,000 emergency dispatchers in 60 countries. The Dave Massengale Memorial Instructor of the Year Award and the Dispatcher of the Year Award are two of the most distinctive awards presented by the IAED. The Instructor of the Year award was created to identify and celebrate individuals who have made the most significant contributions throughout the year to further the education and training of other professionals in the field. The Dispatcher of the Year award was created to identify and celebrate individuals who have made the most significant contributions throughout the year to further the values of IAED and emergency dispatch excellence.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“Emergency dispatchers are the first first responders, the voices we hear in our worst moments when help is needed,” said Pam Stewart, IAED Senior Director of Operations and Board of Certification Chair. “These awards recognize instructors and dispatchers who go above and beyond to answer the call of duty and exemplify excellence under pressure.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">IAED also annually honors Dispatchers of the Year in the U.K. &amp; Ireland, Europe, Asia, the Middle East, China, and Australia.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For more information about the IAED, visit <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"http://www.emergencydispatch.org/\">www.emergencydispatch.org.</a></p>\n\n\n\n<h4 class=\"wp-block-heading\">About the IAED</h4>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The IAED is the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and is the leading body of emergency dispatch experts. Our various boards and councils work on behalf of over 80,000 members in 60 countries—and in coordination with other influential public safety organizations—to ensure that the comprehensive system of emergency dispatching is safe, fast, effective, and cutting-edge. IAED protocols are used in more than 4,216 communications centers across the world and have been time-tested across hundreds of millions of calls for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage dispatching. Learn more at <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"http://www.emergencydispatch.org/\">EmergencyDispatch.org</a>.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">###<br>&nbsp;</p>\n', 'Jody Mader and Danielle King Receive Top Honors  as North America’s 911 Instructor and Dispatcher of the Year', '<p>Given by the IAED, these awards honor a 911 instructor and dispatcher separately for excellence in their roles this year</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', 'd20e2839-06aa-420c-93e4-4359effc18ad', '', '', '2025-04-15 22:50:34', '2025-04-15 22:50:34', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2025/04/15/d20e2839-06aa-420c-93e4-4359effc18ad/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1191, 6, '2025-04-15 22:44:43', '2025-04-15 22:44:43', '\n<h4 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Media contact:&nbsp;</h4>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Matthew Miko&nbsp;<br>(801) 245-0579<br><a href=\"mailto:matthew.miko@emergencydispatch.org\">matthew.miko@emergencydispatch.org</a></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><i><strong>April 15, 2025 (Orlando, FL) </strong></i>— The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAEDTM), the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch services worldwide, today announced that 68 emergency dispatch centers representing medical, fire, police and emergency nurse triage responders in the United States and another 28 centers worldwide have earned or re-earned IAED’s Accredited Center of Excellence (ACE) designation.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Eight of the centers being recognized are local agencies located in Florida. The agencies celebrating their ACE recognition in the fire, medical, or both divisions are Alachua County Sheriff&#8217;s Office, Gainesville; Lake County Board of County Commissioners, Tavares; Lee County Department of Public Safety, Fort Meyers; Manatee County Emergency Communications Center, Bradenton; Miami Beach Public Safety Communications Division, Miami Beach; Polk County Sheriff&#8217;s Office, Winter Haven; Sarasota County Sheriff&#8217;s Office Emergency Operations Bureau, Venice; and Sunstar Paramedics, Largo.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://prioritydispatch.widen.net/s/ncvhbqhdz7/ace-nav-recognition-2025\">&gt;&gt; To view the entire list of recipients by state, click here.</a></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The honorees were recognized during IAED’s 2025 NAVIGATOR conference in Orlando, Florida, which is bringing together more than 1,500 emergency services leaders for continuing education and professional development. Held this year on the same dates as National Public Telecommunicators Week, this marks the biggest week in public safety telecommunications. Attendees will hear keynotes from the author of “The Happiness Equation”, Niel Pasricha, and Emergency Dispatcher resilience expert, Adam Timm. They will also have the chance to tour the Osceola County Sherriff’s Office Comm. Center located in Orlando, FL, a unique opportunity to gain insight into the important and life-saving work done there with the help of The Priority Dispatch System™.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The IAED’s ACE credential is the highest distinction given to emergency communication centers, certifying that they are performing at or above global standards for best practices in emergency response. ACE centers have completed a rigorous set of 20 Points of Accreditation, including strong local oversight, rigorous quality processes, and a commitment to data-driven continuous improvement.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“Accreditation is truly a pinnacle achievement,” said Christof Chwojka, Accreditation Board Chair at IAED. “We applaud the dedicated calltakers, dispatchers, and leaders at each of these agencies for their commitment to the highest standards, which few achieve. The ACE credential means their communities can count on their first first responders to do an outstanding job.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Accreditation is valid for a three-year period, during which all standards must be upheld. Emergency communication centers can earn multiple accreditations, one for each emergency discipline they service (medical, fire, police, and emergency nurse triage). The Priority Dispatch SystemTM &nbsp;is recognized and used in 60 countries as the highest standard of care and practice for emergency dispatch.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For more information about IAED accreditation, visit <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://emergencydistg.wpenginepowered.com/what-we-do/accreditation\">https://emergencydistg.wpenginepowered.com/what-we-do/accreditation.</a></p>\n\n\n\n<h4 class=\"wp-block-heading\">About the IAED</h4>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The IAED is the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and is the leading body of emergency dispatch experts. Our various boards and councils work on behalf of over 80,000 members in 60 countries—and in coordination with other influential public safety organizations—to ensure that the comprehensive system of emergency dispatching is safe, fast, effective and cutting-edge. IAED protocols are used in more than 4,216 communications centers across the world and have been time-tested across hundreds of millions of calls for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage dispatching. Learn more at <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"http://www.emergencydispatch.org/\">EmergencyDispatch.org</a></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">###</p>\n', '8 Florida Emergency Dispatch Centers Earn “Center of Excellence”  Honors from the IAED', '<p>The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAEDTM), the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch services worldwide, today announced that 68 emergency dispatch centers representing medical, fire, police and emergency nurse triage responders in the United States and another 28 centers worldwide have earned or re-earned IAED’s Accredited Center of Excellence (ACE) designation.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', 'ac76580b-53e6-427c-9db8-1d4fad1b5045', '', '', '2025-04-15 22:44:43', '2025-04-15 22:44:43', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2025/04/15/ac76580b-53e6-427c-9db8-1d4fad1b5045/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1192, 6, '2025-04-15 22:39:17', '2025-04-15 22:39:17', '<h4>Media Contact:&nbsp;</h4>\n<p>Matthew Miko&nbsp;<br />(801) 245-0579<br /><a href=\"mailto:matthew.miko@emergencydispatch.org\">matthew.miko@emergencydispatch.org</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><i><strong>April 15, 2025 (Orlando, FL) </strong></i>— The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAEDTM), the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch services worldwide, today announced that 68 emergency dispatch centers representing medical, fire, police and emergency nurse triage responders in the United States and another 28 centers worldwide have earned or re-earned IAED’s Accredited Center of Excellence (ACE) designation.</p>\n<p>The honorees were recognized during IAED’s 2025 NAVIGATOR conference in Orlando, Florida, which is bringing together more than 1,500 emergency services leaders for continuing education and professional development.</p>\n<p><a href=\"C:\\Users\\kelseyr\\Downloads\\NAV2025 - ACE Recognition 1.xlsx\">&gt;&gt; To view the list of recipients by state, click here</a></p>\n<p>The IAED’s ACE credential is the highest distinction given to emergency communication centers, certifying that they are performing at or above global standards for best practices in emergency response. ACE centers have completed a rigorous set of 20 Points of Accreditation, including strong local oversight, rigorous quality processes, and a commitment to data-driven continuous improvement.</p>\n<p>“Accreditation is truly a pinnacle achievement,” said Christof Chwojka, Accreditation Board Chair at IAED. “We applaud the dedicated calltakers, dispatchers, and leaders at each of these agencies for their commitment to the highest standards, which few achieve. The ACE credential means their communities can count on their first first responders to do an outstanding job.”</p>\n<p>Accreditation is valid for a three-year period, during which all standards must be upheld. Emergency communication centers can earn multiple accreditations, one for each emergency discipline they service (medical, fire, police, and emergency nurse triage).</p>\n<p>Over 4,142 emergency communication centers worldwide use the medical, fire, police, and emergency nurse triage protocols developed and maintained by the IAED. The Priority Dispatch SystemTM &nbsp;is recognized and used in 60 countries as the highest standard of care and practice for emergency dispatch.</p>\n<p>For more information about IAED accreditation, visit <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://emergencydistg.wpenginepowered.com/what-we-do/accreditation\">https://emergencydistg.wpenginepowered.com/what-we-do/accreditation</a>.</p>\n<h4>About the IAED</h4>\n<p>The IAED is the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and is the leading body of emergency dispatch experts. Our various boards and councils work on behalf of over 80,000 members in 60 countries—and in coordination with other influential public safety organizations—to ensure that the comprehensive system of emergency dispatching is safe, fast, effective and cutting-edge. IAED protocols are used in more than 4,142 communications centers across the world and have been time-tested across hundreds of millions of calls for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage dispatching. Learn more at <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"http://www.emergencydispatch.org/\">EmergencyDispatch.org</a></p>\n<p>###<br />&nbsp;</p>\n', '68 U.S. Emergency Dispatch Centers Earn “Center of Excellence”  Honors from the IAED', '<p>The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAEDTM), the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch services worldwide, today announced that 68 emergency dispatch centers representing medical, fire, police and emergency nurse triage responders in the United States and another 28 centers worldwide have earned or re-earned IAED’s Accredited Center of Excellence (ACE) designation.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', 'df563883-4a51-425a-8bae-6beac44bba12', '', '', '2025-04-15 22:39:17', '2025-04-15 22:39:17', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2025/04/15/df563883-4a51-425a-8bae-6beac44bba12/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1193, 6, '2025-04-08 17:04:55', '2025-04-08 17:04:55', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">No one ever wants to be in a situation where they need to call 911, but it exists for those terrifying moments of need. In the United States, an <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.nena.org/page/911statistics\">estimated 240 million calls</a> are made to 911 each year, underscoring just how important this helpline is. To better understand the experiences of individuals who have had to call 911, the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch™ (IAED®) conducted a survey, where we found:&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>About 73% of people will call 911 at least once in their lives. </li>\n\n\n\n<li>Over half (59%) of these calls may be lifesaving. </li>\n\n\n\n<li>Most (71%) 911 calls make a difference in the caller’s life. </li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Here’s a closer look at the insights this survey uncovered.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Nearly Three Quarters of Survey Respondents Called 911 At Least Once in Their Lifetime&nbsp;</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">While you may hope you’ll never need to call 911, the data shows that most people will have to at some point. In fact, 73% of our survey respondents called 911 at least once in their lives.&nbsp;<span>&nbsp;</span></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Here’s a closer look at the frequency with which people call 911:&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Over half (54%) of survey respondents have called 911 one to three times in their lives </li>\n\n\n\n<li>About 8% have called four to five times in their lives</li>\n\n\n\n<li>About 10% have called more than five times in their lives</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Methods of Contacting 911&nbsp;</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Throughout this article, we’ll be using the phrase, “calling 911.” However, we’d like to quickly note that calling 911 isn’t the only way to get in contact with the emergency helpline.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">According to our survey, here’s how people most often contact 911:&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>About 94% use a phone call <span> </span></li>\n\n\n\n<li>About 4% use text messaging </li>\n\n\n\n<li>About 1% use a video call </li>\n\n\n\n<li>A near 0% amount of people contact 911 through TTY/TDD</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Over Half of Survey Respondents Agree to Some Extent that Their 911 Call Saved a Life&nbsp;</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Many 911 dispatchers would likely agree that their job can be challenging and stressful at times. But what they might not always realize is just how vital their role is. In fact, over half (59%) of survey respondents agreed to some extent that their 911 call saved a life. That means dispatchers could be saving the lives of their community members about every other phone call they take.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">When asked to what extent they agreed with the statement, “My phone call to 911 resulted in saving a life,” survey respondents said the following:&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>About 23% strongly agreed </li>\n\n\n\n<li>About 18% agreed </li>\n\n\n\n<li>About 19% somewhat agreed </li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The rest of survey respondents were either neutral in their response (20%) or disagreed to some extent (21%).&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Nearly Three Quarters of Survey Respondents Agree to Some Extent that Their 911 Call Made a Difference in Their Lives&nbsp;</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Beyond saving lives, 911 dispatchers make a meaningful impact. In fact, 71% of survey respondents agreed to some extent that their call to 911 made a difference. When asked how much they agreed with the statement, “A phone call to 911 has made a difference in my life,” survey respondents said the following:&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>About 29% strongly agreed </li>\n\n\n\n<li>About 28% agreed </li>\n\n\n\n<li>About 15% somewhat agreed </li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The remaining survey respondents were either neutral in their responses (19%) or disagreed to some extent (10%).&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Of Respondents Who Remembered Feeling Fear or Anxiety While Calling, 82% Felt Calmer After Calling 911&nbsp;</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Calling 911 is often a stressful experience, but by and large, dispatchers keep the public calm. In fact, about eight out of ten callers who felt fear or anxiety reported feeling calmer to some extent after calling. When asked how calling 911 affected their fear or anxiety during the emergency, respondents said:&nbsp;<span> &nbsp;</span></p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>About 28% had their fear or anxiety significantly reduced </li>\n\n\n\n<li>About 42% had their fear or anxiety somewhat reduced</li>\n\n\n\n<li>About 12% did not have their fear or anxiety reduced </li>\n\n\n\n<li>About 4% had their fear or anxiety increased </li>\n\n\n\n<li>About 7% answered I don’t know/I can’t remember </li>\n\n\n\n<li>About 9% reported never feeling any fear or anxiety </li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">About 83% of Survey Respondents Have Some Confidence in Their Community’s 911 Dispatchers&nbsp;</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Given that dispatchers can have an incredible impact on the people they speak with, it’s important that the public feels positively about dispatchers and the help they provide. Luckily, this overwhelmingly seems to be the case. When asked how much they agreed with the statement, “My experience with 911 gives me confidence my community is safe in the hands of our dispatchers,” respondents said the following:&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>About 32% strongly agreed </li>\n\n\n\n<li>About 37% agreed </li>\n\n\n\n<li>About 14% somewhat agreed </li>\n\n\n\n<li>About 13% neither agreed nor disagreed</li>\n\n\n\n<li>About 1% somewhat disagreed </li>\n\n\n\n<li>No respondents disagreed </li>\n\n\n\n<li>About 3% strongly disagreed </li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Methodology&nbsp;</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">This survey was conducted by the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch (IAED) through SurveyMonkey to understand the experiences and perceptions of individuals who have called 911. The sample included U.S. adults aged 18 to 99+, with gender and age distribution balanced to reflect U.S. Census demographics. Participants were surveyed across all regions of the United States. With a margin of error of +/- 5.447%, the results provide a reliable snapshot of public sentiment regarding 911 services, the role of emergency dispatchers, and the impact of these calls on emergency situations.</p>\n', '911 Calls Make a Difference in 71% of People’s Lives', '<p>In the United States, an estimated 240 million calls are made to 911 each year, underscoring just how important this helpline is. To better understand the experiences of individuals who have had to call 911, the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch™ (IAED®) conducted a survey.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', '8bd6527c-6d61-4ba5-880c-2067e5b5d2d5', '', '', '2025-04-08 17:04:55', '2025-04-08 17:04:55', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2025/04/08/8bd6527c-6d61-4ba5-880c-2067e5b5d2d5/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1194, 6, '2025-03-13 20:36:55', '2025-03-13 20:36:55', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch<span><sup>TM</sup></span> (IAED®) is proud to announce the launch of the new Emergency Dispatch Learning Portal, now live and ready to enhance the training and certification process for over 69,000 Emergency Dispatchers worldwide. This innovative platform is designed to ensure that our members have ready access to certification courses and a wide variety of trainings to help them upskill year over year, while providing a streamlined learning management process for emergency call center leaders and training supervisors.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Enhancing Public Safety</strong> Emergency Dispatchers are the first, first responders, gathering critical information and providing potentially lifesaving instructions to callers while medical, police, and fire responders are en route. With such an important role, the IAED requires that they meet rigorous certification and continuing dispatch education (CDE) requirements to ensure they’re well-prepared to use the IAED’s protocols to handle any emergency in their community efficiently and effectively.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Highest Level of Service</strong> &#8220;We’re excited to introduce a platform that more fully addresses the needs of our users,&#8221; said Matt Miko, Director of Academics, Research, and Communications for the IAED. “The tens of thousands of courses that are taken yearly mean our learning portal needs to make it easy to onboard, train, and keep Emergency Dispatchers certified. This ensures that our certified Emergency Dispatchers are always ready to provide the highest level of service to the public.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Potentially Lifesaving Instructions</strong> Along with learning how to follow the protocols in collecting critical information for responders, Emergency Dispatchers learn how to deliver the precise, potentially lifesaving instructions to callers while coordinating with medical, police, and fire responders. From giving CPR instructions to delivering a baby, administering Narcan to identifying a stroke, the IAED Protocols can help keep the caller alive while ground responders are on the way.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">###</p>\n\n\n\n<h4 class=\"wp-block-heading\">About the IAED:&nbsp;</h4>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch is the standard-setting organization for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and is the leading body of emergency services dispatch experts. Our various Boards and Councils work on behalf of over 69,000 members in 60 countries—and in coordination with other influential public safety organizations—to ensure that the comprehensive system covering all aspects of emergency dispatching is safe, fast, effective, and cutting-edge. IAED protocols are used in more than 4,216 communication centers across the world and have been time-tested across hundreds of millions of calls for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage dispatching.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">&nbsp;</p>\n', 'IAED Launches New Emergency Dispatch Learning Portal', '<p>The International Academies of Emergency DispatchTM (IAED®) is proud to announce the launch of the new Emergency Dispatch Learning Portal, now live and ready to enhance the training and certification process for over 69,000 Emergency Dispatchers worldwide.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', 'c6b127f8-500d-453c-8448-0b6140844f53', '', '', '2025-03-13 20:36:55', '2025-03-13 20:36:55', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2025/03/13/c6b127f8-500d-453c-8448-0b6140844f53/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1195, 6, '2025-02-04 17:45:04', '2025-02-04 17:45:04', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Ty Wooten, Director of Governmental Affairs<br>801-363-9127<br><a href=\"mailto:ty.wooten@emergencydispatch.org\">ty.wooten@emergencydispatch.org</a></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Salt Lake City, UT, USA</strong> ─ The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAED™) is proud to announce its partnership with the U.S. Department of Transportation (USDOT) as an Ally in Action, supporting the National Roadway Safety Strategy (NRSS). This collaboration underscores the IAED’s commitment to reducing roadway fatalities and improving safety for all American drivers.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Following 10 consecutive quarters of declining roadway fatalities, the USDOT unveiled its 2025 NRSS Progress Report and introduced 15 new commitments from organizations like the IAED to further the mission of reversing the crisis on America’s roads. As a global leader in setting standards and accreditation for public safety emergency dispatch, the IAED brings its expertise to this vital initiative through innovative protocols, data analysis, and advocacy efforts.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The IAED has worked to develop additional Post-Dispatch Instructions in its police, fire, and medical protocols relating to moving vehicles to a safe location after an initial crash. These instructions aim to reduce secondary crashes and improve the safety of responders and the public.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch is proud to join the U.S. Department of Transportation’s Allies in Action,” said Ty Wooten, IAED’s Director of Governmental Affairs. “Every 911 call impacts our nation’s transportation system, and 911 professionals play a critical role in ensuring the right response and providing life-saving Pre-Arrival Instructions.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In collaboration with the National 911 Program, housed in NHTSA’s Office of EMS, the IAED is also evaluating data from over 50 million emergency calls to gain insights into roadway-related incidents. This study will identify the proportion of 911 calls tied to roadway emergencies and inform the USDOT’s Safe Systems Approach to roadway safety.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The IAED also continues to champion reducing the use of lights-and-siren by first responders, promoting safer and more effective response practices. There is a statistically significant relationship between lights-and-siren responses and increased crashes on the road. One study found that an ambulance involved in a fatal crash was twice as likely to be running in emergency mode than not. By decreasing lights-and-siren responses, we can create safer roads for Americans.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-center wp-block-paragraph\"># # #</p>\n\n\n\n<h4 class=\"wp-block-heading\">About the IAED:</h4>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch is the standard-setting organization for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and is the leading body of emergency services dispatch experts. Our various Boards and Councils work on behalf of over 69,000 members in 59 countries—and in coordination with other influential public safety organizations—to ensure that the comprehensive system covering all aspects of emergency dispatching is safe, fast, effective, and cutting-edge. IAED protocols are used in more than 4,142 communication centers across the world and have been time-tested across hundreds of millions of calls for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage dispatching. Learn more at &nbsp;<a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://emergencydistg.wpenginepowered.com/home\">emergencydispatch.org</a></p>\n\n\n\n<h4 class=\"wp-block-heading\">About the National Roadway Safety Strategy (NRSS):</h4>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The National Roadway Safety Strategy (NRSS) is a comprehensive plan initiated by the U.S. Department of Transportation (USDOT) to address the critical issue of roadway fatalities and injuries across America. Focused on the Safe Systems Approach, the NRSS emphasizes shared responsibility among policymakers, industry leaders, and the public to create safer roadways. By implementing evidence-based strategies, fostering innovation, and promoting collaboration, the NRSS aims to reverse the crisis on America’s roads and achieve significant reductions in traffic-related deaths.&nbsp;</p>\n', 'IAED Partners with USDOT to Enhance Roadway Safety as an Ally in Action', '<p>The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAED™) is proud to announce its partnership with the U.S. Department of Transportation (USDOT) as an Ally in Action, supporting the National Roadway Safety Strategy (NRSS).</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', '65ba45b7-5c50-4d01-ba00-c8f55864befc', '', '', '2025-02-04 17:45:04', '2025-02-04 17:45:04', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2025/02/04/65ba45b7-5c50-4d01-ba00-c8f55864befc/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1196, 6, '2025-01-09 16:48:15', '2025-01-09 16:48:15', '\n<h4 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Contact for IAED</h4>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Pam Stewart, Senior Director of Operations<br>(<span data-teams=\"true\">385) 429-9391</span><br><a href=\"mailto:pam.stewart@emergencydispatch.org\">pam.stewart@emergencydispatch.org</a></p>\n\n\n\n<h4 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Contact for Priority Dispatch</h4>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Chris Murdock, President<br>(385) 429-8097<br><a href=\"mailto:chris.murdock@prioritydispatch.net\">chris.murdock@prioritydispatch.net</a></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Charlotte, NC, USA</strong> – Last month, OnStar was recognized by the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAED™) for achieving its fifth consecutive Medical Accreditation as an Accredited Center of Excellence (ACE). This landmark accomplishment marks their fifteenth anniversary of becoming ACE, an achievement that takes a significant amount of hard work.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The ACE recertification process involves meeting a rigorous set of 20 Points of Accreditation, including strong local oversight, rigorous quality processes, and a commitment to data-driven continuous improvement. Accreditation lasts for a period of three years, during which time all standards must be upheld.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Becoming an ACE-certified agency in a single discipline—whether police, fire, or medical dispatch—is a significant accomplishment. However, achieving triple accreditation in all three disciplines, known as Tri-ACE, is truly a remarkable feat that reflects unparalleled dedication, expertise, and a commitment to excellence in emergency dispatch.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In April 2024, OnStar made history as the first and only private, non-911 entity to achieve Tri-ACE status.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Ruby Hilton, Assistant Manager of OnStar’s Emergency Quality and Analytics, highlights the work necessary to become a tri-ACE with the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“Tri-ACE serves as a promise to OnStar callers that we will deliver the highest levels of care and professionalism, with a sense of urgency,” Hilton explains. “Reaching ACE in one discipline requires significant time, effort and resources, and we worked hard and are proud to have accomplished this across all three disciplines.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">This latest Medical ACE accreditation not only reflects OnStar’s dedication to upholding IAED standards but also highlights the company’s long-standing role as a trusted partner in emergency response. Chris Murdock, the President of Priority Dispatch Corporation, affirms this point, noting:</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“Achieving and maintaining accreditation is a testament to OnStar’s unwavering commitment to excellence and innovation in emergency response. This milestone underscores the dedication of OnStar’s team to uphold the highest standards in public safety.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">To celebrate its achievements, OnStar will be recognized at the U.S. NAVIGATOR Conference in April 2025 in Orlando, Florida, where leaders in emergency dispatch gather to honor excellence and innovation in the field.</p>\n\n\n\n<h4 class=\"wp-block-heading\">About the IAED</h4>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch is the standard-setting organization for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and is the leading body of emergency services dispatch experts. Our various Boards and Councils work on behalf of over 69,000 members in 59 countries—and in coordination with other influential public safety organizations—to ensure that the comprehensive system covering all aspects of emergency dispatching is safe, fast, effective, and cutting-edge. IAED protocols are used in more than 4,142 communication centers across the world and have been time-tested across hundreds of millions of calls for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage dispatching. Learn more at &nbsp;<a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://emergencydispatch.org\">emergencydispatch.org</a></p>\n\n\n\n<h4 class=\"wp-block-heading\">About Priority Dispatch</h4>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Priority Dispatch™ is the world leader in providing research-based protocol solutions to emergency call centers in medical, fire, police, and nurse triage disciplines. For over 44 years, EMS and 911 agencies have used the Medical Priority Dispatch System™ (MPDS®), initially with cardsets and now only fully represented in ProQA software. Our solutions have been time-tested in 30 languages across hundreds of millions of calls and serve communities in thousands of agencies in 59 countries. Get to know us at &nbsp;<a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://prioritydispatch.net\">prioritydispatch.net</a></p>\n', 'OnStar Recognized for Fifth Medical Accreditation, Celebrating 15 Years of Excellence', '<p>Last month, OnStar was recognized by the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAED™) for achieving its fifth consecutive Medical Accreditation as an Accredited Center of Excellence (ACE).</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', '35e1c01e-6a42-4417-9281-b16d4d7749ef', '', '', '2025-01-09 16:48:15', '2025-01-09 16:48:15', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2025/01/09/35e1c01e-6a42-4417-9281-b16d4d7749ef/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1197, 6, '2024-11-26 21:26:48', '2024-11-26 21:26:48', '\n<p class=\"PageNumberFooter wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE: NOVEMBER 26, 2024</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Contact for IAED</strong><br>Pam Stewart, Senior Director of Operations<br>800.363.9127 ext. 131<br><a href=\"mailto:pam.stewart@emergencydispatch.org\">pam.stewart@emergencydispatch.org</a></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Contact for Priority Dispatch</strong><br>Brian Dale, President <br>385.236.4561<br><a href=\"mailto:brian.dale@prioritydispatch.net\">brian.dale@prioritydispatch.net</a></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">SALT LAKE CITY, UT<i>&nbsp;</i>– Priority Dispatch® and the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch are proud to announce the release of FPDS<code>®</code> v8.0, the landmark update of the Fire Priority Dispatch System™ (FPDS®) designed to bring critical enhancements to emergency fire dispatch. This significant evolution modernizes and expands the FPDS protocols, equipping Emergency Fire Dispatchers with powerful tools to meet the demands of today’s complex fire and rescue environments.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">With input from users around the world, FPDS v8.0 features updates to 32 of 34 protocols, including new instructions for managing emerging threats like electric vehicle fires and extreme weather incidents. Two new protocols—Protocol H, for on-scene medical assessment and treatment, and Protocol V, for assisting callers trapped in vehicles on fire—have been added to enhance civilian and responder safety during high-risk events.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">&#8220;We’re incredibly grateful to the agencies who submitted over 210 Proposals for Change based on their real-world experience with the FPDS,” said Mike Thompson, Chair of the IAED™ Curriculum Board and member of the Fire Standards Council. “Over 150 of their proposals were included, helping us refine the FPDS to address the growing complexities and dangers of fire/rescue incidents.<span>&nbsp; </span>This update will enable dispatchers to develop more precise information and give expanded instructions to help keep their communities safe and ensure Fire Departments have the information they need to respond.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">This release also introduces a revised HAZMAT protocol, enabling dispatchers to provide civilian-directed decontamination guidance in specific situations and offers expanded options for callers in sinking vehicles. Protocol H, based on the standards from the Medical Priority Dispatch System™ (MPDS®), equips fire dispatchers with medical instructions for essential interventions, such as bleeding control and airway positioning, enhancing response during critical moments.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“FPDS v8.0 represents a new era in fire dispatch, building on the best ideas from both our dedicated users and our team of protocol experts,” said Brian Dale, Former Salt Lake City Fire Chief and President of Priority Dispatch. “We’re thrilled to offer this update to our community of users across the world, empowering them to provide the best possible response to every call.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-center wp-block-paragraph\">###</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><span><strong>About</strong></span><span><strong>&nbsp;</strong></span><span><strong>the</strong></span><span><strong>&nbsp;</strong></span><span><strong>IAED:</strong></span></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch is the standard-setting organization for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and is the leading body of emergency services dispatch experts. Our various Boards and Councils work on behalf of over 69,000 members in 59 countries—and in coordination with other influential public safety organizations—to ensure that the comprehensive system covering all aspects of emergency dispatching is safe, fast, effective, and cutting-edge. IAED protocols are used in more than 4,142 communication centers across the world and have been time-tested <span>across hundreds of millions of calls for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage dispatching. Learn more at&nbsp;</span><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://emergencydistg.wpenginepowered.com/home\"><span>emergencydispatch.org</span></a></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><span><strong>About</strong></span><span><strong>&nbsp;</strong></span><span><strong>Priority</strong></span><span><strong>&nbsp;</strong></span><span><strong>Dispatch:</strong></span></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><span>Priority Dispatch</span><span>™</span><span>&nbsp;</span><span>is the world leader in providing research-based protocol solutions to emergency call centers in medical, fire, police, and nurse triage disciplines. For over 44 years, EMS and 911 agencies have used the Medical Priority Dispatch System</span><span>™</span><span>&nbsp;</span><span>(MPDS</span><span>®</span><span>), initially with cardsets and now only</span><span>&nbsp;</span><span>fully</span><span>&nbsp;</span><span>represented</span><span>&nbsp;</span><span>in</span><span>&nbsp;</span><span>ProQA</span><span>&nbsp;</span><span>software.</span><span>&nbsp;</span><span>Our</span><span>&nbsp;</span><span>solutions</span><span>&nbsp;</span><span>have</span><span>&nbsp;</span><span>been</span><span>&nbsp;</span><span>time-tested</span><span>&nbsp;</span><span>in</span><span>&nbsp;</span><span>30</span><span>&nbsp;</span><span>languages</span><span>&nbsp;</span><span>across hundreds of millions of calls and serve communities in thousands of agencies in 59 countries. Get to know us at&nbsp;</span><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.prioritydispatch.net/en\"><span>prioritydispatch.net</span></a></p>\n', 'IAED RELEASES LANDMARK EVOLUTION OF EMERGENCY FIRE PROTOCOLS WITH FPDS V8.0', '<p>FPDS V8.0 is designed to bring critical modern enhancements to emergency fire dispatch, equipping Emergency Fire Dispatchers with powerful tools to meet the demands of today’s complex fire and rescue environments.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', 'd3271550-cfad-4132-90be-24116bd42964', '', '', '2024-11-26 21:26:48', '2024-11-26 21:26:48', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2024/11/26/d3271550-cfad-4132-90be-24116bd42964/', 0, 'post', '', 0);
INSERT INTO `wp_posts` (`ID`, `post_author`, `post_date`, `post_date_gmt`, `post_content`, `post_title`, `post_excerpt`, `post_status`, `comment_status`, `ping_status`, `post_password`, `post_name`, `to_ping`, `pinged`, `post_modified`, `post_modified_gmt`, `post_content_filtered`, `post_parent`, `guid`, `menu_order`, `post_type`, `post_mime_type`, `comment_count`) VALUES
(1198, 6, '2024-09-19 19:26:13', '2024-09-19 19:26:13', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE: 09/19/2024</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Dave Warner</strong><br><strong>Police Protocol, Academics, and Standards Expert</strong><br><strong>International Academies of Emergency Dispatch&nbsp;</strong><br><strong>dave.warner@emergencydispatch.org</strong><br><strong>801-759-3277</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Across the United States, small towns are facing a resource crisis: a severe shortage of police officers, forcing many to make the difficult decision to disband their police departments. In Minnesota towns like Goodhue and Morris as well as Washburn, Illinois and others; the lack of adequate staffing has led to the shuttering of local police forces, leaving communities to rely on county sheriffs or neighboring towns for law enforcement. This trend is troubling, as it not only diminishes the sense of security in these towns but also threatens their vibrancy and overall quality of life.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Small towns like Goodhue have been hit particularly hard by the nationwide police shortage. With officer resignations and retirements surging, many departments simply can&#8217;t keep up. In Goodhue, the police chief and his remaining officers resigned en masse after warning that without better pay and benefits, the town would never be able to attract new recruits. This scenario is playing out across the country, with at least 12 small towns disbanding their police departments in just the past two years due to similar issues<span>​</span> (<a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.yahoo.com/news/americas-small-towns-disbanding-police-111321308.html\">Yahoo</a>)<span>​</span> (<a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.police1.com/police-administration/articles/small-towns-adjusting-to-life-without-police-depts-RqpeNYFhUbYZtYNQ/\">Police1</a>).</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The impact of these closures is profound. Residents in towns without local police forces may experience longer response times and a loss of the familiar, trusted presence of local officers. This can lead to increased anxiety among residents and a decline in the overall sense of community.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">One part of a potential solution that could help keep these small-town police departments operational and, by extension, keep the towns themselves vibrant: the Priority Dispatch System&#8217;s Police Priority Dispatch System (PPDS) Version 7. This advanced dispatch system is designed to maximize the efficiency of law enforcement resources, which is crucial in a time when those resources are dwindling.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">PPDS v7 offers a unique feature: OMEGA pathways for non-urgent, low-priority calls. This allows emergency dispatchers to manage these calls effectively without immediately dispatching officers to the scene. Instead, they can guide callers through alternative response options, such as online reporting, referrals to other agencies,<span>&nbsp;</span>or by referring callers to online reporting systems which is gaining momentum in policing as a method for the public to file official police reports. By handling these non-emergency calls within the dispatch system, PPDS v7 ensures that officers are available for more critical situations, thereby optimizing the use of limited personnel<span>​</span> (<a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.police1.com/police-administration/articles/small-towns-adjusting-to-life-without-police-depts-RqpeNYFhUbYZtYNQ/\">Police1</a>).</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For small towns struggling to keep their police departments afloat, adopting a system like PPDS v7 could be part of the solution to maintaining local law enforcement services. By reducing the burden on officers and allowing them to focus on high-priority incidents, towns can stretch their limited resources further, potentially preventing the closure of their police department and keeping these communities safe, secure, and vibrant.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>About Priority Dispatch Corp.:</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Priority Dispatch™ is the world leader in providing research-based protocol solutions to emergency call centers in medical, fire, police, and nurse triage disciplines. For over 44 years, EMS and 911 agencies have used the Medical Priority Dispatch System™ (MPDS®), initially with cardsets and now only fully represented in ProQA software. Our solutions have been time-tested in 30 languages across hundreds of millions of calls and serve communities in thousands of agencies in 59 countries. Get to know us at&nbsp;<a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://prioritydispatch.net/\">prioritydispatch.net</a>.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>About</strong><i><strong>&nbsp;</strong></i><strong>International Academies of Emergency Dispatch:</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch is the standard-setting organization for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and is the leading body of emergency services dispatch experts. Our various Boards and Councils work on behalf of over 69,000 members in 59 countries—and in coordination with other influential public safety organizations—to ensure that the comprehensive system covering all aspects of emergency dispatching is safe, fast, effective, and cutting-edge. IAED protocols are used in more than 4,131 communication centers across the world and have been time-tested across hundreds of millions of calls for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage dispatching. Learn more at&nbsp;<a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://emergencydistg.wpenginepowered.com/home\">emergencydispatch.org</a>.</p>\n', 'Disappearing Small Town Police Departments Threaten the Vibrancy of Main Street, USA: How PPDS v7 Can Help Amidst Staffing Crises', '<p>Across the United States, small towns are facing a resource crisis: a severe shortage of police officers, forcing many to make the difficult decision to disband their police departments.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', 'a4d54282-cf22-4e03-80fd-0c05d12cb14c', '', '', '2024-09-19 19:26:13', '2024-09-19 19:26:13', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2024/09/19/a4d54282-cf22-4e03-80fd-0c05d12cb14c/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1199, 6, '2024-09-12 23:24:04', '2024-09-12 23:24:04', '\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE: 09/12/2024</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Wendy Whittaker</strong><br><strong>Content and Communications Manager</strong><br><strong>Priority Dispatch</strong><br><strong>wendy.whittaker@prioritydispatch.net</strong><br><strong>(541)280-7172</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>DOVER, DELAWARE: </strong>August 27, 2024, was an eventful day for emergency medical responders across the globe. That day, at about 3:36 PM, the Dover Police Department <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://doverpolice.org/2024/09/12/heroic-efforts-by-dover-police-communications-operators-save-lives-in-european-waters/\">received word</a> of a distressed ship off the coast of England from a caller in Albania. While the caller meant to alert authorities in Dover, England, they mistakenly called the Dover, Delaware, agency in the United States.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The phone call was answered by Communications Operator MacKenzie Atkinson, who had been working at the Dover Police Department for just under a year. Understanding the risks of disconnecting from the caller, MacKenzie followed recent training she had received from <i>Priority Dispatch</i> and the protocols from the <i>International Academies of Emergency Dispatch</i> then using the Fire Priority Dispatch System (FPDS) to collect life-saving information from the caller. The Dover Police Department went live with FPDS on August 7, 2024, meaning less than a month had passed since implementation before the agency received the mis-routed international distressed ship call.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Of the training and protocols, Emergency Communications Manager, Matthew Carey, said “Oh, I&#8217;m a huge advocate of it. I’m a big believer in it. I think it makes it easier for everyone … And honestly, if it wasn&#8217;t for this EFD [Emergency Fire Dispatcher] training, I don&#8217;t know if MacKenzie would know the right questions to ask.”&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">While C/O MacKenzie used FPDS <strong>Protocol 73 &#8211; Watercraft in Distress/Collision </strong>to collect vital information about coordinates and more, C/O Connor Logan began alerting international authorities. It took under four minutes to make contact with international agencies such as The French Coast Guard, His Majesty’s Coast Guard of England, the Dover Police Station in England, and more. It took about ten minutes more before the Maritime and Coastguard Agency confirmed they were enroute to rescue the vessel.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“From the time we took the call, we had help started internationally in 15 minutes and 48 seconds … 15 minutes [is] not a long time, but it is a long time. &#8230; We had over 15 complaints during that time period that were just local complaints,” said Matthew Carey.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">While MacKenzie and Connor worked to save the international sinking vessel, C/O Patricia Mazzola and C/O Briana Dozier&nbsp;handled domestic calls to ensure there were no delays, keeping the Dover Police Department in full operation.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">President of Priority Dispatch, Brian Dale had this to say about the incident in Dover: “<span class=\"ui-provider a b c d e f g h i j k l m n o p q r s t u v w x y z ab ac ae af ag ah ai aj ak\" data-teams=\"true\" dir=\"ltr\">Our EFD training and protocols know no borders. The Emergency Dispatchers in Dover, Delaware gave life-saving instructions until the proper authorities were on scene to complete the rescue. So well done by the entire Dover, Delaware team. Hip Hip Hurrah!”</span></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">When asked what he’d like the public to know about this event, Matthew Carey responded with accolades for his dispatch team, saying, “They&#8217;re extraordinary. They step up to the plate every single day.”&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">On September 5, 2024, the Dover Police Department received word that the ship and all those aboard were safely returned to the Port of Dover, England due to the heroic efforts of MacKenzie, Connor, Patricia, and Briana that day. To honor the team’s efforts, Matthew Carey has nominated them to receive a Dover PD&nbsp;<i>Distinguished Unit Commendation.</i></p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>About Dover Police Department:</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">&nbsp;It is the mission of the Dover Police Department to proudly serve the community by protecting the life and property of its citizens, enforcing the law, and working in partnership with the public to enhance the quality of life and nurture public trust by holding ourselves to the highest standards of performance and ethics. Learn more at <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://doverpolice.org/\">doverpolice.org</a>.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>About Priority Dispatch Corp.:</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Priority Dispatch™ is the world leader in providing research-based protocol solutions to emergency call centers in medical, fire, police, and nurse triage disciplines. For over 44 years, EMS and 911 agencies have used the Medical Priority Dispatch System™ (MPDS®), initially with cardsets and now only fully represented in ProQA software. Our solutions have been time-tested in 30 languages across hundreds of millions of calls and serve communities in thousands of agencies in 59 countries. Get to know us at <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://prioritydispatch.net\">prioritydispatch.net</a></p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>About</strong><i><strong> </strong></i><strong>International Academies of Emergency Dispatch:</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch is the standard-setting organization for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and is the leading body of emergency services dispatch experts. Our various Boards and Councils work on behalf of over 69,000 members in 59 countries—and in coordination with other influential public safety organizations—to ensure that the comprehensive system covering all aspects of emergency dispatching is safe, fast, effective, and cutting-edge. IAED protocols are used in more than 4,131 communication centers across the world and have been time-tested across hundreds of millions of calls for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage dispatching. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://emergencydistg.wpenginepowered.com/home\">emergencydispatch.org</a></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">&nbsp;</p>\n', 'A Tale of Two Dovers: A Trans-Atlantic Rescue', '<p>August 27, 2024, was an eventful day for emergency medical responders across the globe. That day, at about 3:36 PM, the Dover Police Department received word of a distressed ship off the coast of England from a caller in Albania. While the caller meant to alert authorities in Dover, England, they mistakenly called the Dover, Delaware, agency in the United States.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', 'a01bd4d3-b15b-4cb6-832b-3d0212807069', '', '', '2024-09-12 23:24:04', '2024-09-12 23:24:04', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2024/09/12/a01bd4d3-b15b-4cb6-832b-3d0212807069/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1200, 6, '2024-06-13 19:31:35', '2024-06-13 19:31:35', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Salt Lake City, Utah</strong> – In response to the alarming rise in mental health distress calls to 911 – specifically suicide calls – the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch®(IAED™) and Priority Dispatch®announce the release ofProtocol 41: Caller in Crisis (1stParty Only). This protocol and its accompanying specialized training are the first and only in the industry to address first-person callers with suicide risk and ideation. Developed in partnership with leading experts in this area,including Dr. James Marshall and the 911 Training Institute, Protocol 41: Caller in Crisis (1stParty Only) is a critical tool that empowers Emergency Dispatchers to handle these difficult calls with confidence. Knowing this protocol and its specific training will create the opportunity for the best possible outcomes. The Protocol’s interrogation process provides crucial information to help ensure that appropriately trained mental health professionals are more often involved in the public safety response and subsequent priority care.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>The COVID-19</strong> pandemic and following years have have brought unprecedented challenges to individuals’ mental well-being. As anxiety and depression have surged, so too have suicide risk and ideation calls to emergency communication centers everywhere in the world. Compounding this issue has been the impact of these calls on the mental well-being of Emergency Dispatchers themselves and overall agency morale. The interrogation process allows agencies to better respond with the appropriately trained mental health professionals armed and trained in this novel protocol.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Priority Dispatch’s president and former Salt Lake City Fire Department Chief, Brian Dale, expressed the company’s commitment to addressing this issue, stating, “Our agencies have asked for enhancements to training, protocols, and more appropriate response capabilities to better manage the influx of mental health calls. They need focused training and specific tools to help improve outcomes for these callers, and also to help boost confidence and reduce anxiety and distress when handling these calls.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Dr. Jeff Clawson, Medical Director and co-founder of the IAED, spoke to the development of Protocol 41: Caller in Crisis (1st Party Only), saying, “We have looked to the world’s experts and real data to make sure Emergency Dispatchers are always prepared for these most challenging situations. When presented with a Caller in Crisis, this Protocol and training provides the Emergency Dispatcher with specific calming and control techniques to work through a host of behaviors often presented during the call. In addition, when faced with an imminent suicide threat patient, the Emergency Dispatcher will use specific pre-arrival instructions to reduce the risk of serious injury or death – while making the scene safer for responders.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Protocol 41: </strong>Caller in Crisis (1stParty Only) is the latest of the medical, fire, and police protocols developed by the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch, and is available for use in communication centers through Priority Dispatch’s software solution, ProQA®Paramount, the logic and inference engine for the Medical Priority Dispatch System™(MPDS®). Previously, callers with mental distress have been handled on Protocol 25: Psychiatric/Abnormal Behavior/Suicide Attempt, which was recently significantly updated to incorporate the latest research and procedures. Now second- and third-party mental distress callers will still be handled on Protocol 25, with first-person callers presenting suicide risk and ideation handled on Protocol 41: Caller in Crisis (1stParty Only).</p>\n', 'WORLD’S FIRST SUICIDE RISK AND IDEATION PROTOCOL FOR 1ST PARTY CALLERS WILL HELP MANAGE THE RECORD AND INCREASING NUMBER OF 911 MENTAL DISTRESS CALLS', '<p>In response to the alarming rise in mental health distress calls to 911 – specifically suicide calls – the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAED™) and Priority Dispatch® announce the release of Protocol 41: Caller in Crisis (1st Party Only).</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', 'f6900411-0b58-4655-9621-478b905c087c', '', '', '2024-06-13 19:31:35', '2024-06-13 19:31:35', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2024/06/13/f6900411-0b58-4655-9621-478b905c087c/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1201, 6, '2024-04-16 16:25:53', '2024-04-16 16:25:53', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">April 16, 2024 (NATIONAL HARBOR, MD) — The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch<sup>®</sup> (IAED<sup>TM</sup>), the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch services worldwide, today announced that 103 emergency dispatch centers representing medical, fire, police and emergency nurse triage responders have earned or re-earned IAED’s Accredited Center of Excellence (ACE) designation.  </p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The honorees were recognized during IAED’s 2024 NAVIGATOR conference in National Harbor, Maryland, which is bringing together more than 1,300 emergency services leaders this week for continuing education and professional development.   </p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">>>Click here to view the list of <a href=\"https://prioritydispatch.widencollective.com/assets/share/asset/hbvnn37wzj\">recipients by state</a>.   </p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The IAED’s ACE credential is the highest distinction given to emergency communication centers, certifying that they are performing at or above global standards for best practices in emergency response. ACE centers have completed a rigorous set of 20 Points of Accreditation, including strong local oversight, rigorous quality processes, and a commitment to data-driven continuous improvement. </p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“Accreditation is truly a pinnacle achievement,” said Christof Chwojka, Accreditation Board Chair at IAED. “We applaud the dedicated calltakers, dispatchers, and leaders at each of these agencies for their commitment to the highest standards, which few achieve. The ACE credential means their communities can count on their first first responders to do an outstanding job.”&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Accreditation is valid for a three-year period, during which all standards must be upheld. Emergency communication centers can earn multiple accreditations, one for each emergency discipline they service (medical, fire, police, and emergency nurse triage).   </p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Over 4,142 emergency communication centers worldwide use the medical, fire, police, and emergency nurse triage protocols developed and maintained by the IAED. The Priority Dispatch SystemTM  is recognized and used in 59 countries as the highest standard of care and practice for emergency dispatch.   </p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For more information about IAED accreditation, visit <a href=\"https://emergencydistg.wpenginepowered.com/what-we-do/accreditation\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://emergencydistg.wpenginepowered.com/what-we-do/accreditation</a>.&nbsp;</p>\n', '103 U.S. Emergency Dispatch Centers Earn “Center of Excellence”  Honors from the IAED', '<p>The IAED announced today that 103 emergency dispatch centers representing medical, fire, police and emergency nurse triage responders have earned or re-earned IAED’s Accredited Center of Excellence (ACE) designation in 2024. The honorees were recognized during NAVIGATOR conference in National Harbor, Maryland, which is bringing together more than 1,300 emergency services leaders this week.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', '45775273-da81-4697-b718-0c7ba29cfd9c', '', '', '2024-04-16 16:25:53', '2024-04-16 16:25:53', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2024/04/16/45775273-da81-4697-b718-0c7ba29cfd9c/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1202, 6, '2024-04-16 13:35:45', '2024-04-16 13:35:45', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><i><strong>April 16, 2024 (NATIONAL HARBOR, MD) </strong></i>— Miranda Rosso, Chief Training Officer with Jefferson County, CO, Communications Center Authority, Lakewood, Colorado, USA, is being honored today as North America’s 911 Dispatcher of the Year by the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch<sup>®</sup> (IAED<sup>TM</sup>), the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and the leading body of emergency dispatch experts.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The award, given annually to an emergency dispatcher who exemplifies excellence in his or her work throughout the year, is being announced at IAED’s 2024 <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://navigator.emergencydispatch.org/home\">NAVIGATOR</a> Conference in National Harbor. The annual conference brings together more than 1,650 emergency services leaders from across the U.S. and the world to stay abreast of best practices in emergency dispatch protocols.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Miranda was hired to work with Jeffcom in January 2023 and was still in the training stage when she took a call about a person trapped by a tree that had come through the skid steer he was operating. The quality assurance (QA) committee that reviewed the call noted, “This was a difficult call for even the most experienced calltaker; however, Miranda went above and beyond to do everything she could to assist while maintaining a level of professionalism and compassion that was remarkable.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Miranda has won several lifesaver awards and her peers and supervisors have recognized her throughout the year for her performance on various calls, praising not only her accuracy and efficiency, but also the way she talks to each caller, saying, “Miranda absolutely shines in emergency dispatch and has recently become a CTO to help others shine as well.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">As a non-profit with worldwide reach, IAED provides training, education, and certification for more than 70,000 emergency dispatchers in over 59 countries. The Dispatcher of the Year Award is one of the most distinctive awards presented by IAED, created to identify and celebrate individuals who have made the most significant contributions throughout the year to further the values of IAED and emergency dispatch excellence.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“IAED is proud of every calltaker and dispatcher who works as the voice of reassurance for a caller on the other end of the line, but there are a few individuals who stand out every year, exemplifying excellence under pressure,” said Pam Stewart, IAED Senior Director of Operations and Certification Board Chair.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">IAED also annually honors Dispatchers of the Year in the U.K. &amp; Ireland, Europe, Asia, the Middle East, China, and Australia.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For more information about IAED, visit <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.emergencydispatch.org\">www.emergencydispatch.org</a>.</p>\n', 'Miranda Rosso of Lakewood, Colorado, Honored as  North America’s 911 Dispatcher of the Year', '<p>Miranda Rosso, Chief Training Officer with Jefferson County, CO, Communications Center Authority, Lakewood, Colorado, USA, is being honored today as North America’s 911 Dispatcher of the Year by the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAEDTM), the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and the leading body of emergency dispatch experts.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', '5a69bc20-da93-495b-b370-9e806c32a817', '', '', '2024-04-16 13:35:45', '2024-04-16 13:35:45', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2024/04/16/5a69bc20-da93-495b-b370-9e806c32a817/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1203, 6, '2024-04-16 13:29:58', '2024-04-16 13:29:58', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><i><strong>April 16, 2024 (NATIONAL HARBOR, MD) </strong></i>— Mike “Doog” Dolan of New Orleans, LA, is being honored today as the 2024 Dave Massengale Memorial Instructor of the Year by the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch<sup>®</sup> (IAED<sup>TM</sup>), the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and the leading body of emergency dispatch experts.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The award, given annually to a North American emergency dispatch instructor who exemplifies excellence in his or her work throughout the year, is being announced at the IAED’s 2024 <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://navigator.emergencydispatch.org/home\">NAVIGATOR</a> &nbsp;Conference in National Harbor. The annual conference brings together more than 1,650 emergency services leaders from across the country and the world to stay abreast of best practices in emergency dispatch protocols.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“Mike Dolan has been instructing emergency medical dispatch [EMD] since 2005 and is one of only two IAED instructors who teaches emergency fire and police, as well,” said Eric Fayad, IAED Associate Director of Instructor Services. “He’s animated and really engages students even remotely, he’s stellar at that. And his reach has been incredible – over the 19 years he’s been an instructor, he has influenced the lives of almost 6,000 students. That means that in one year he touches around 78 million 911 calls. He was an EMT and paramedic for 36 years with the New York State Troopers, as well as a fire chief, and the breadth of experience and knowledge he brings to the classroom is invaluable.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">As a non-profit with worldwide reach, the IAED provides training, education, and certification for more than 70,000 emergency dispatchers in over 59 countries. The Dave Massengale Memorial Instructor of the Year Award is one of the most distinctive awards presented by the IAED, created to identify and celebrate individuals who have made the most significant contributions throughout the year to further the education and training of other professionals in the field.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“Emergency dispatchers are the <i>first</i> first responders, the voices we hear in our worst moments when help is needed,” said Pam Stewart, IAED Senior Director of Operations and Board of Certification Chair. “The Instructor of the Year Award recognizes instructors who go above and beyond to train emergency dispatchers, so that they are well equipped, prepared and confident.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For more information about the IAED, visit <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.emergencydispatch.org\">www.emergencydispatch.org</a>.</p>\n', 'Mike Dolan of New Orleans  Honored as the Dave Massengale Memorial Instructor of the Year 2024', '<p>Mike “Doog” Dolan of New Orleans, LA, is being honored today as the 2024 Dave Massengale Memorial Instructor of the Year by the IAED. The award is given annually to a North American emergency dispatch instructor who exemplifies excellence in his or her work.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', '4178ac70-d768-433b-8b4e-76fef1319d4a', '', '', '2024-04-16 13:29:58', '2024-04-16 13:29:58', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2024/04/16/4178ac70-d768-433b-8b4e-76fef1319d4a/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1204, 6, '2024-04-01 22:57:20', '2024-04-01 22:57:20', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>For Immediate Release: 03/19/2024</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>El Paso County, Colorado: </strong>A staple course of the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch has made its way to Calhan High School in the Pikes Peak BOCES District. The Emergency Telecommunicator™ (ETC™) Course provides introductory training for a wide range of concepts and skills needed to successfully work in a public safety communications center.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">With a shortage of dispatchers in almost every state in the US, this high school ETC Course comes at a critical time.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“I’d love to see ETC become a recruitment tool for agencies,” said Bonni Stockman, the Instructor Program Coordinator for the IAED who is spearheading ETC efforts in high schools. “The goal is for students to have jobs waiting for them.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Connecting students to local agencies is crucial to the long-term success of these programs, by helping students see the industry in a more hands-on way and giving them insights to what a career in public safety might entail.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“This is a great entry point for 911,” Stockman said. “It’s also a great place to work for high school graduates due to hours that allow the pursuit of higher education while working, which is a win-win for both center and employee.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Salaries for Emergency Dispatchers vary across the nation, with Indeed.com reporting a median salary of about $50,000, Salary.com reporting a median salary of about $43,000, and ZipRecruiter.com reporting a median salary of about $53,000 a year. Calhan’s ETC Instructor reports Emergency Dispatcher salaries of around $60,000 a year.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Calhan High School isn’t the first school to adopt this pilot program. Schools around the US have had success with implementing ETC Courses. What sets El Paso County apart is help from El Paso-Teller E9-1-1 Authority in making connections between the school and communications centers, granting students better opportunities and higher job placement.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Stockman views what’s happening with ETC in high schools not just as a potential recruiting tool, but as a cost-saving measure. “While there are things that can’t be trained outside of the center, there are many that can. What could a center do with the additional time and money saved for each high school ETC graduate they hire?”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Some agencies have expressed worries that the age of these graduates might be a barrier to performing the rigorous work the job requires. Stockman says her experience is that anyone with the right mindset shouldn’t be turned away.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“My advice to centers is to not forget this generation,” Stockman said. “They can do this job as well as anyone.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For any parties interested in learning more about the IAED’s ETC Course, a remote option is available at <a href=\"https://courses.prioritydispatch.net/\">https://courses.prioritydispatch.net/</a>. The ETC course gives interested parties all of the knowledge and tools necessary to enter the industry of emergency telecommunications.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Watch <a href=\"https://www.foxbusiness.com/economy/colorado-high-school-starts-911-dispatcher-class-during-nationwide-shortage\">FOX coverage in Colorado</a> of the&nbsp;Calhan High School in the Pikes Peak BOCES District.</p>\n', 'ETC Course Trains High School Students to Fill Nationwide Emergency Dispatcher Shortage', '<p>With a shortage of dispatchers in almost every state in the US, this high school ETC Course comes at a critical time. The Emergency Telecommunicator™ (ETC™) Course provides introductory training for a wide range of concepts and skills needed to successfully work in a public safety communications center.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', '8cb30c42-b450-457e-9f4d-97e4a3d2af23', '', '', '2024-04-01 22:57:20', '2024-04-01 22:57:20', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2024/04/01/8cb30c42-b450-457e-9f4d-97e4a3d2af23/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1205, 6, '2024-03-12 20:20:40', '2024-03-12 20:20:40', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><span>FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE</span></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">March 12, 2024, Salt Lake City&nbsp;– For decades, Emergency Medical Dispatchers completed standard CPR training every two years to maintain their certification. However, this changed in April 2022 when the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAED™) launched a first-of-its-kind, Dispatcher-Directed CPR (DD-CPR) course, specifically designed for the non-visual, high stakes environment of emergency dispatch.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Setting a new standard in online training, this course features a range of practice scenarios in a fully simulated ProQA®&nbsp;environment, ensuring that Emergency Dispatchers gain not just the knowledge, but also the experience of using IAED Pre-Arrival Instructions correctly in these critical events.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Today, less than two years later, the IAED is proud to announce that the DD-CPR course has been successfully completed by over 20,000 certified Emergency Medical Dispatchers.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“Today marks a red-letter day for the IAED in leading the charge for Emergency Dispatchers to follow a single, unified standard when handling calls involving sudden cardiac arrest while providing best-in-class online training to make this happen,” said Jeff Clawson, M.D, Chair of the Rules Group of the IAED Medical Council of Standards and Medical Director of the Research, Standards, and Academics Division.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“When we launched this course, we saw the need to use the latest technology for Emergency Dispatchers with simulations that build on their knowledge while also reinforcing how to identify and handle these types of situations when seconds count,” said Jerry Overton, IAED President. &nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">According to the CDC, every year in the United States more than 356,000 people experience an out-of-hospital cardiac arrest (OHCA) and 60% to 80% die without reaching the hospital. This only underscores the importance of Emergency Dispatchers being prepared to handle OHCA calls.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“Emergency Dispatchers operate in the non-visual realm,” Overton added. “The ongoing training and practice provided through our DD-CPR course is critical to ensuring readiness to deliver life-saving CPR directions at any time, day or night.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Dr. Clawson has often stated, &#8220;Remember in cardiac arrest, Emergency Dispatchers are truly the ‘first,’ first responders with an unbeatable ‘zero-minute’ response time. These now more precisely trained professionals are there the second the phone stops ringing—they’re just there by remote control.&#8221;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The DD-CPR course can be completed in less than two hours and is available at no cost to certified members of the IAED and now satisfies the biyearly recertification requirement in a much more useful and meaningful way. </p>\n', 'OVER 20,000 EMERGENCY MEDICAL DISPATCHERS UPSKILLED  WITH DISPATCHER-DIRECTED CPR TRAINING', '<p>The IAED is proud to announce that the DD-CPR course has been successfully completed by over 20,000 certified Emergency Medical Dispatchers. Setting a new standard in online training, this course features a range of practice scenarios in a fully simulated ProQA® environment.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', '2fcf3836-384d-4c70-b000-c034d9578eac', '', '', '2024-03-12 20:20:40', '2024-03-12 20:20:40', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2024/03/12/2fcf3836-384d-4c70-b000-c034d9578eac/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1206, 6, '2024-01-22 15:36:37', '2024-01-22 15:36:37', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>UPDATE ON LAPSED CERTIFICATION POLICY</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In the past, accumulating enough Continuing Dispatch Education (CDE) hours could be challenging. With literally hundreds of lessons and courses now available on the College of Emergency Dispatch, we’re proud to say that’s no longer the case.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Effective July 1, 2024</strong>, the IAED is eliminating its Lapsed Certification Policy. Formerly, this policy allowed for members to recertify after their membership had lapsed under certain circumstances, which included paying fees, taking new CDE courses, and taking recertification exams.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The removal of this policy simply means that every member who wishes to retain their certification must recertify before the expiration date. Failure to do so will result in the need for the member to retake the relevant certification course.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>AS A REMINDER:</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Academy certification is valid for two years, unless revoked or suspended.</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Recertification applications can be accepted as early as six months before expiration.</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Auto-generated recertification reminder emails are sent out at regular intervals for the convenience of Academy Members.</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Please make sure your email address is current in your Member Record.</li>\n\n\n\n<li>While the Academy will make efforts to send out recertification reminders to all its members, it is ultimately the responsibility of the member to track their own expiration dates.</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">We value the responsibility and accountability of our members. Thank you for your continued excellence and assuming the professionalism of maintaining one’s honored standing in such an important and life-sustaining role. </p>\n', 'IAED Will Eliminate Its Lapsed Certification Policy on July 1, 2024', '<p>Effective July 1, 2024, the IAED is eliminating its Lapsed Certification Policy.<br />\nThe removal of this policy simply means that every member who wishes to retain their certification must recertify before the expiration date. Failure to do so will result in the need for the member to retake the relevant certification course.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', 'b36c30c1-0608-44fd-8eff-179b9cb2386b', '', '', '2024-01-22 15:36:37', '2024-01-22 15:36:37', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2024/01/22/b36c30c1-0608-44fd-8eff-179b9cb2386b/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1207, 6, '2023-11-16 23:09:02', '2023-11-16 23:09:02', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><span>The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch™ (IAED<strong><sup>®</sup></strong>) is pleased to announce the groundbreaking Protocol 40: Incident Standby/Staging, along with comprehensive updates to the Police Priority Dispatch System™ (PPDS<strong><sup>®</sup></strong>), Fire Priority Dispatch System™<sup> </sup>(FPDS<strong><sup>®</sup></strong>), and Medical Priority Dispatch System™ (MPDS<strong><sup>®</sup></strong>) protocols.</span></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><span><strong>Protocol 40: Incident Standby/Staging</strong></span></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><span>Developed by the IAED, Protocol 40 addresses the diverse standby requests EMS agencies receive without reported patients at the scene. This protocol empowers agencies to efficiently process, assign, and document varied responses to each unique request for service. ProQA v5.1.1.48 includes a Special Procedures Briefing for Protocol 40. </span></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><span><strong>MPDS v14.0.137</strong><strong><span><span>&nbsp;(10/18/2023)</span></span></strong></span></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><span>The Medical Priority Dispatch System sees significant updates in version 14.0.137. The clarifiers related to SERIOUS hemorrhage have been refined, and Protocol 23: Overdose/Poisoning now includes new answer choices and suffixes to distinguish between accidental and intentional substance intake.</span></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><span><strong>FPDS v7.1.244 </strong><strong><span><span>(9/28/2023)</span></span></strong></span></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><span>The Fire Priority Dispatch System receives notable adjustments in version 7.1.244, enhancing Case Entry definitions, Chief Complaint Selection Rules, and refining wording for various Key Questions in Protocol 69: Structure Fire. These improvements contribute to clearer communication and more accurate responses in fire-related emergencies.</span></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><span><strong>PPDS v7.0.348 </strong><strong><span><span>(10/27/2023)</span></span></strong></span></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><span>The Police Priority Dispatch System version 7.0.348 includes the addition of two agency-defined Critical EPD Information (CEIs) for every Chief Complaint. Protocol 106: Assault/Sexual Assault/Shooting/Stabbing now enables suffixes E and U for all STABBING codes, offering more detailed coding options.</span></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><span>The IAED remains committed to supporting emergency dispatchers and responders as they serve their communities. These protocol updates reflect our dedication to continual evolution, ensuring the highest standard of care. The release includes training, certification, and ongoing education to empower Emergency Dispatchers, enhancing efficiency, safety, and community confidence.</span></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"> <span>For the full email communication regarding these release details, click <span>here</span>.</span></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><span><strong>About the IAED</strong></span></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><span>The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch<sup>®</sup> (IAED™) is a nonprofit, standard-setting organization promoting safe and effective emergency dispatch services worldwide. The IAED has over 40 protocol-based research studies published in peer-reviewed journals and provides over 304,281 active medical, police, fire, and nurse dispatch certifications globally. The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch is the leading body of emergency services dispatch experts with various Boards and Councils that work on behalf of over 69,000 members in 59 countries—and in coordination with other influential public safety organizations—to ensure that the comprehensive system covering all aspects of emergency dispatching is safe, fast, effective, and cutting-edge. IAED protocols are used in more than 4,080 communication centers across the world and have been time-tested across hundreds of millions of calls for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage dispatching.</span></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">&nbsp;</p>\n', 'IAED Unveils Protocol 40 and Enhanced Protocols Across Police, Fire, and Medical Disciplines', '<p>The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch™ (IAED®) is pleased to announce the groundbreaking Protocol 40: Incident Standby/Staging, along with comprehensive updates to the Police Priority Dispatch System™ (PPDS®), Fire Priority Dispatch System™ (FPDS®), and Medical Priority Dispatch System™ (MPDS®) protocols.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', 'e2d70224-3afd-4cb7-b3ca-481b5f0eff2b', '', '', '2023-11-16 23:09:02', '2023-11-16 23:09:02', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2023/11/16/e2d70224-3afd-4cb7-b3ca-481b5f0eff2b/', 0, 'post', '', 0);
