(1208, 6, '2023-06-23 16:25:47', '2023-06-23 16:25:47', '\n<p class=\"has-text-align-center wp-block-paragraph\"><strong><em>To help address America’s growing 911 staffing crisis, IAED’s Mentor Program helps rookie dispatchers gain confidence and experience while being coached by a senior mentor</em></strong></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong><em>June 22, 2023 (SALT LAKE CITY, Utah)—</em></strong>America’s 911 dispatchers are the “heroes on the headsets,” responding to millions of emergency calls across the United States every year. Yet there can be a steep learning curve for rookies on the job—one that results in newly hired 911 dispatchers leaving the job in significant numbers every year.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In response, and to help strengthen 911 dispatch centers across the country, the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch (IAED), which sets the best practices for emergency dispatch and response management around the world, today announced the U.S. launch of its highly regarded Emergency Medical Dispatcher (EMD) Mentor Certification Course. The course’s goal is to deepen experienced EMDs’ understanding of the IAEDs standards and protocols while giving them tools and teaching them to be excellent mentors and coaches. EMD Mentors then sit side-by-side with new EMDs and provide hands-on insights, feedback, and support during those critical first months at the console.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“The IAED is proud to set the standards for 911 protocols around the world, and the EMD Mentor Program is a natural extension of the best practices we are bringing into dispatch centers worldwide,” said IAED Board of Certification Chair Pam Stewart.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><u>According to a recent study</u> jointly conducted by IAED and the National Association of State 911 Administrators (NASNA), one of the greatest challenges facing 911 centers today is adequate staffing. Many centers reported significant attrition as new hires faltered due to the stressful demands of the job. IAED’s Mentor course was specifically designed to address these issues and to support a one-on-one training approach for a faster and higher quality ramp-up for rookie dispatchers.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In one recent example, during the second call taken by a dispatcher who was new to the job, a man called 911, panicked and desperate for help. His wife was in active labor in their car on the side of the highway. With guidance and coaching from the experienced mentor, the new dispatcher was able to locate the caller using a mapping system to identify local landmarks.&nbsp;With the calm expertise of the senior mentor coaching the dispatcher through the call, the dispatcher gave directions to the man on how to deliver the baby safely while help was on the way. Within one minute of identifying the caller’s location, the baby was born in the car.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Said the rookie dispatcher after the fact: “My mentor’s role in supporting and guiding me through this first-time experience allowed me, in turn, to support the caller, manage the patient condition and calm the caller’s anxiety.&nbsp;I would never have processed the call as smoothly or safely without the input from my mentor.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Another recent participant says she was quite daunted by the thought of putting everything she had learned into practice. “My mentor was a dispatcher who had <em>years </em>of experience. She was calm, quietly confident and reassuring when I needed her. She also knew when to push me to fly solo when I was ready.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Adds IAED’s Stewart: “We are passionate about equipping our <em>first</em>, first responders with the best practices, tools, and resources to deliver the highest standards in emergency dispatch services. We are proud to support the 911 profession by providing this vital mentoring course to help new EMDs gain experience, confidence, and ultimately contribute positively to the profession for years to come.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">To learn more about IAED’s 911 Emergency Medical Dispatcher Mentor Course email <a href=\"mailto:info@prioritydispatch.net\">info@prioritydispatch.net</a>&nbsp;or download the&nbsp;<a href=\"https://prioritydispatch.widen.net/s/mk89w6tbbr/mentor-course-info-sheet\">info sheet</a>.<br>\n&nbsp;</p>\n', 'IAED Launches 911 Dispatcher Mentoring Program', '<p>As part of the growing 911 staffing problem, many 911 centers are experiencing significant attrition as new hires falter due to the stressful demands of the job. IAED’s Mentor course was specifically designed to address these issues and to support a one-on-one training approach for a faster and higher quality ramp-up for rookie dispatchers.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', '15aa42c9-2d9f-43b2-9e6d-f8bbe7aaa2f2', '', '', '2023-06-23 16:25:47', '2023-06-23 16:25:47', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2023/06/23/15aa42c9-2d9f-43b2-9e6d-f8bbe7aaa2f2/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1209, 6, '2023-06-21 00:09:57', '2023-06-21 00:09:57', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Report finds more than one-third of nation’s call centers significantly understaffed with call demand surging</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">SALT LAKE CITY (June 12, 2023) – The results from a recently released <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://emergencydistg.wpenginepowered.com/in-the-news/press-releases/d1ff0c4c-38ca-4e4f-8a96-cb6d9f2e5f63\">survey</a> of the workforce staffing emergency call centers are sobering. Of 774 9-1-1 call centers around the United States, 36% reported vacancy rates that exceeded 30% of staffing levels, with 166 of those centers reporting rates above 40%.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The highest vacancy rate reported in 2022 actually reached 83%. The 25 centers reporting the greatest losses are located in all regions of the country, and the average vacancy rate – 25 percent – is similar across all sizes of agencies.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The International Academies or Emergency Dispatch (IAED) and the National Association of State 9-1-1 Administrators (NASNA) released this week the updated report<i>, America’s 911 Workforce In Crisis</i>. Survey responses reveal that 9-1-1 staffing shortages are affecting all parts of the country and all sizes of call centers.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“When there are more incoming demands on the 9-1-1 center than there are trained, qualified personnel to respond, public safety is put at risk,” said Ty Wooten, director of government affairs for the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch (IAED).</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">An accompanying analysis of news stories from around the country in the first half of 2023 found more than a dozen communities reeling from staffing vacancies from Maine to Hawaii, and more are being reported nearly every week.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The workforce crisis is occurring at a time when 9-1-1 telecommunicators are fielding more calls each year. Among the findings:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>36% of call centers reported having fewer positions filled in 2022 than in 2019.</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Nearly one-third reported stunning vacancy rates in 2022: 13 reported that 70% or more of positions were unfilled; 92 reported a vacancy rate between 50-69%; and 166 reported rates of 30% to 49%.</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Nearly 4,000 staff departures were recorded in one year collectively among the centers. Departing staff with at least one year of experience cited pay, work hours, and stress as reasons for leaving.</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Only 27% of centers reported no change in actual staffing levels over the most recent 4-year period.</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“An alarming number of 911 centers are experiencing their own workforce emergencies,” said Harriet Rennie-Brown, executive director of NASNA. “We need better workforce recruitment, retention, and support to ensure the quality of the 911 emergency services we all rely on.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Workforce challenges are expected to be a major topic of discussion at the upcoming annual conference for NENA – The 9-1-1 Association in Grapevine, Texas, where Wooten will be speaking about the subject on June 19.</p>\n\n\n\n<div>\n<p><i><strong>America’s 911 Workforce In Crisis</strong></i><strong> report is available at </strong><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://emergencydistg.wpenginepowered.com/in-the-news/press-releases/d1ff0c4c-38ca-4e4f-8a96-cb6d9f2e5f63\"><strong>www.EmergencyDispatch.org under News.</strong></a><strong> A graphic of the range of stunning vacancy rates is available.</strong></p>\n</div>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><i>IAED is the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and is the leading body of emergency dispatch experts. Learn more at </i><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.EmergencyDispatch.org\"><i>www.EmergencyDispatch.org</i></a><i>.</i></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><i>The National Association of State 911 Administrators (NASNA) is the voice of the states on public policy issues impacting 911. Learn more at </i><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.nasna.org\"><i>nasna911.org</i></a><i>.</i></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Review and download the&nbsp;<a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://prioritydispatch.widen.net/s/kmwkvgqfdx/iaed_nasna_staffing-survey-report-2023\">full survey report</a>&nbsp;for all of the details.</p>\n', 'The Hidden Emergency Crisis: 9-1-1 Staffing Is a Challenge in Many Communities', '<p>The results from a recently released survey of the workforce staffing emergency call centers are sobering. Of 774 9-1-1 call centers around the United States, 36% reported vacancy rates that exceeded 30% of staffing levels, with 166 of those centers reporting rates above 40%.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', '963b3265-c780-447d-a040-053cd7c51958', '', '', '2023-06-21 00:09:57', '2023-06-21 00:09:57', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2023/06/21/963b3265-c780-447d-a040-053cd7c51958/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1210, 6, '2023-06-20 21:59:28', '2023-06-20 21:59:28', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A new report on the IAED and NASNA&#8217;s 911 Staffing Survey revealing the crisis in America&#8217;s 911 centers suggests that to recruit and retain qulified 911 applicants, public safety agencies must improve working conditions, and that the public and policymakers need to respect 911 professionals alongside their peers in public safety.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><a href=\"https://prioritydispatch.widen.net/s/kmwkvgqfdx/iaed_nasna_staffing-survey-report-2023\">America&#8217;s 911 Workforce in Crisis Survey Report</a></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"></p>\n', 'America&#8217;s 911 Workforce in Crisis: Survey Report', '<p>A new report on the IAED and NASNA&#8217;s 911 Staffing Survey revealing the crisis in America&#8217;s 911 centers recommends that public safety agencies try to improve working conditions and find more ways to recruit and retain qualified 911 applicants.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', 'd1ff0c4c-38ca-4e4f-8a96-cb6d9f2e5f63', '', '', '2023-06-20 21:59:28', '2023-06-20 21:59:28', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2023/06/20/d1ff0c4c-38ca-4e4f-8a96-cb6d9f2e5f63/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1211, 6, '2023-04-18 15:48:52', '2023-04-18 15:48:52', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><i><strong>April 18, 2023 (DENVER, Col.) </strong></i>— The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAED), the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch services worldwide, today announced that 87 emergency dispatch centers representing medical, fire, police and emergency nurse triage responders have earned or re-earned IAED’s Accredited Center of Excellence (ACE) designation.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The honorees were recognized during IAED’s 2023 NAVIGATOR conference in Denver, Colorado, which is bringing together more than 1,500 emergency services leaders for continuing education and professional development.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://prioritydispatch.widen.net/s/qkt9lbbmfk/ace-and-re-ace-list-2023\">&gt;&gt; View the list of recipients by state</a></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The IAED’s ACE credential is the highest distinction given to emergency communication centers, certifying that they are performing at or above global standards for best practices in emergency response. ACE centers have completed a rigorous set of 20 Points of Accreditation, including strong local oversight, rigorous quality processes, and a commitment to data-driven continuous improvement.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><span>“Accreditation is truly a pinnacle achievement,” said Christof Chwojka, Accreditation Board Chair at IAED. “We applaud the dedicated calltakers, dispatchers, and leaders at each of these agencies for their commitment to the highest standards, which few achieve. The ACE credential means their communities can count on their </span><i><span>first</span></i><span> first responders to do an outstanding job.”</span></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><span>Accreditation is valid for a three-year period, during which all standards must be upheld. Emergency communication centers can earn multiple accreditations, one for each emergency discipline they service (medical, fire, police, and emergency nurse triage).</span></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><span>Over 3,500 emergency communication centers worldwide use the medical, fire, police, and emergency nurse triage protocols developed and maintained by the IAED. The Priority Dispatch System<sup>TM&nbsp; </sup>is recognized and used in 46 countries as the highest standard of care and practice for emergency dispatch.</span></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For more information about IAED accreditation, visit <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://emergencydistg.wpenginepowered.com/what-we-do/accreditation\">https://emergencydistg.wpenginepowered.com/what-we-do/accreditation</a>.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>About the IAED</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The IAED is the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and is the leading body of emergency dispatch experts. Our various boards and councils work on behalf of over 69,000 members in 59 countries—and in coordination with other influential public safety organizations—to ensure that the comprehensive system of emergency dispatching is safe, fast, effective and cutting-edge. IAED protocols are used in more than 4,080 communications centers across the world and have been time-tested across hundreds of millions of calls for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage dispatching. Learn more at <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.emergencydispatch.org\">www.emergencydispatch.org</a>.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-center wp-block-paragraph\">###</p>\n', '87 U.S. Emergency Dispatch Centers Earn “Center of Excellence”  Honors from the IAED', '<p>April 18, 2023 (DENVER, Col.) — The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAED), the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch services worldwide, today announced that 87 emergency dispatch centers representing medical, fire, police and emergency nurse triage responders have earned or re-earned IAED’s Accredited Center of Excellence (ACE) designation.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', '878347ea-8c31-405f-a6a9-1e0ea980b9bd', '', '', '2023-04-18 15:48:52', '2023-04-18 15:48:52', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2023/04/18/878347ea-8c31-405f-a6a9-1e0ea980b9bd/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1212, 6, '2023-04-18 15:43:39', '2023-04-18 15:43:39', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The Colorado honorees are: &nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Denver 911 in Denver, CO  </li>\n\n\n\n<li>Colorado Springs Public Safety Communications in Colorado Springs, CO</li>\n\n\n\n<li>El Paso County Sheriff’s Office in Colorado Springs, CO    </li>\n\n\n\n<li>Fort Collins 911 in Fort Collins, CO</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Loveland Emergency Communications Center in Loveland, CO</li>\n\n\n\n<li>South Metro Fire Rescue in Centennial, CO</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Weld County Regional Communications Center in Greeley, CO</li>\n\n\n\n<li></li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">These agencies were recognized during IAED’s 2023 <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://navigator.emergencydispatch.org/home\">NAVIGATOR</a> conference in Denver, which is bringing together more than 1,500 emergency services leaders for continuing education and professional development.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://emergencydistg.wpenginepowered.com/in-the-news/press-releases/8429ee86-cb0b-4350-8bf3-1932d7ab0fe2\">&gt;&gt; View the list of recipients by state</a></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The IAED’s ACE credential is the highest distinction given to emergency communication centers, certifying that they are performing at or above global standards for best practices in emergency response. ACE centers have completed a rigorous set of 20 Points of Accreditation, including strong local oversight, rigorous quality processes, and a commitment to data-driven continuous improvement.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><span>“Accreditation is truly a pinnacle achievement,” said Christof Chwojka, Accreditation Board Chair at IAED. “We applaud the dedicated calltakers, dispatchers, and leaders at each of these agencies for their commitment to the highest standards, which few achieve. The ACE credential means Coloradans can count on their </span><i><span>first</span></i><span> first responders to do an outstanding job.”</span></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><span>Accreditation is valid for a three-year period, during which all standards must be upheld. Emergency communication centers can earn multiple accreditations, one for each emergency discipline they service (medical, fire, police, and emergency nurse triage).</span></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><span>Over 3,500 emergency communication centers worldwide use the medical, fire, police, and emergency nurse triage protocols developed and maintained by the IAED. The Priority Dispatch System<sup>TM&nbsp; </sup>is recognized and used in 46 countries as the highest standard of care and practice for emergency dispatch.</span></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For more information about IAED accreditation, visit <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://emergencydistg.wpenginepowered.com/what-we-do/accreditation\">https://emergencydistg.wpenginepowered.com/what-we-do/accreditation</a>.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>About the IAED</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The IAED is the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and is the leading body of emergency dispatch experts. Our various boards and councils work on behalf of over 69,000 members in 59 countries—and in coordination with other influential public safety organizations—to ensure that the comprehensive system of emergency dispatching is safe, fast, effective and cutting-edge. IAED protocols are used in more than 4,080 communications centers across the world and have been time-tested across hundreds of millions of calls for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage dispatching. Learn more at <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.emergencydispatch.org\">www.emergencydispatch.org</a>.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-center wp-block-paragraph\">###</p>\n', 'Seven Colorado Emergency Response Centers Earn  “Center of Excellence” Honors from the IAED', '<p>April 18, 2023 (DENVER, Col.) — The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAED), the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch services worldwide, today announced that seven emergency dispatch centers in Colorado, staffed by teams of medical, fire, police, and emergency nurse triage responders, have earned or re-earned IAED’s Accredited Center of Excellence (ACE) designation.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', '2b5e896f-d914-47dc-8d3b-304e6e2ad5a7', '', '', '2023-04-18 15:43:39', '2023-04-18 15:43:39', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2023/04/18/2b5e896f-d914-47dc-8d3b-304e6e2ad5a7/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1213, 6, '2023-04-18 15:21:46', '2023-04-18 15:21:46', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><i><strong>April 18, 2023 (DENVER, Col.) </strong></i>— Stephen Zipprich, Senior Emergency Communications Operator at the Bureau of Communications (911) in Portland, Oregon is being honored today as North America’s 911 Dispatcher of the Year by the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAED), the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and the leading body of emergency dispatch experts.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The award, given annually to an emergency dispatcher who exemplifies excellence in his or her work throughout the year, is being announced at IAED’s 2023 <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://navigator.emergencydispatch.org/home\">NAVIGATOR</a> Conference in Denver. The annual conference brings together more than 1,500 emergency services leaders from across the U.S. and the world to stay abreast of best practices in emergency dispatch protocols.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“When we started implementing new emergency response protocols in May of 2021, staff m orale was low, but Stephen was a shining light through it all with his positive attitude and desire to advance emergency communications,” said Ryan DesJardins, Quality Assurance Manager with the Bureau of Emergency Communications (911). “He has the advanced skill of marrying great call-taking with compliant protocol use. He demonstrates great customer service, compassion, and professionalism, and he uses the protocol appropriately to optimize outcomes and keep callers, patients, and responders safe. </p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“Stephen is an example to his peers and doesn&#8217;t hesitate to jump in and help his fellow dispatchers,” DesJardins added.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">As a non-profit with worldwide reach, IAED provides training, education, and certification for more than 70,000 emergency dispatchers in over 50 countries. The Dispatcher of the Year Award is one of the most distinctive awards presented by IAED, created to identify and celebrate individuals who have made the most significant contributions throughout the year to further the values of IAED and emergency dispatch excellence.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“IAED is proud of every calltaker and dispatcher who works as the voice of reassurance for a caller on the other end of the line. But there are a few individuals who stand out every year, exemplifying excellence under pressure,” said Pam Stewart, IAED Senior Director of Operations and Certification Board Chair.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">IAED also annually honors Dispatchers of the Year in the U.K. &amp; Ireland, Europe, Asia, the Middle East, China, and Australia.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For more information about IAED, visit <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.emergencydispatch.org\">www.emergencydispatch.org</a>.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>About the IAED</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The IAED is the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and is the leading body of emergency dispatch experts. Our various boards and councils work on behalf of over 69,000 members in 59 countries—and in coordination with other influential public safety organizations—to ensure that the comprehensive system of emergency dispatching is safe, fast, effective and cutting-edge. IAED protocols are used in more than 4,080 communications centers across the world and have been time-tested across hundreds of millions of calls for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage dispatching. Learn more at <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.emergencydispatch.org\">www.emergencydispatch.org</a>.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">&nbsp;</p>\n', 'Stephen Zipprich of Portland, Oregon Honored as  North America’s 911 Dispatcher of the Year', '<p>Award honors an exemplary emergency dispatcher for professional excellence</p>\n<p>April 18, 2023 (DENVER, Col.) — Stephen Zipprich, Senior Emergency Communications Operator at the Bureau of Communications (911) in Portland, Oregon is being honored today as North America’s 911 Dispatcher of the Year by the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAED), the standard-setting organization for emerg</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', '08bc63b6-a134-4547-8794-be994386d8ab', '', '', '2023-04-18 15:21:46', '2023-04-18 15:21:46', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2023/04/18/08bc63b6-a134-4547-8794-be994386d8ab/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1214, 6, '2023-04-18 15:12:00', '2023-04-18 15:12:00', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><i><strong>April 18, 2023 (DENVER, Col.) </strong></i>— Barbara Ireland, Deputy Chief, retired, New Orleans EMS, is being honored today as North America’s 911 Instructor of the Year by the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAED), the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and the leading body of emergency dispatch experts.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The award, given annually to a U.S. emergency dispatch instructor who exemplifies excellence in his or her work throughout the year, is being announced at the IAED’s 2023 <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://navigator.emergencydispatch.org/home\">NAVIGATOR</a> &nbsp;Conference in Denver. The annual conference brings together more than 1,500 emergency services leaders from across the country and the world to stay abreast of best practices in emergency dispatch protocols.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“Barbara Ireland has been instructing emergency medical dispatch [EMD] since 2008, during which time she has instructed more than 250 classes,” said Eric Fayad, IAED Associate Director of Instructor Services. “She was quick to jump into remote instruction at the start of the pandemic, and she has since been appointed a senior instructor, tasked with mentoring new EMD instructor candidates. She also is one of the leading EMD Study Hall instructors, taking extra time to help students with review. Barbara is a fierce proponent of emergency protocols and is not afraid to take on additional assignments when needed.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">As a non-profit with worldwide reach, the IAED provides training, education, and certification for more than 70,000 emergency dispatchers in over 50 countries. The Instructor of the Year Award is one of the most distinctive awards presented by the IAED, created to identify and celebrate individuals who have made the most significant contributions throughout the year to further the education and training of other professionals in the field.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“Emergency dispatchers are the <i>first</i> first responders, the voices we hear in our worst moments when help is needed,” said Pam Stewart, IAED Senior Director of Operations and Board of Certification Chair. “The Instructor of the Year Award recognizes instructors who go above and beyond to train emergency dispatchers, so that they are well equipped, prepared and confident.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For more information about the IAED, visit <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.emergencydispatch.org\">www.emergencydispatch.org</a>.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>About the IAED</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"> The IAED is the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and is the leading body of emergency dispatch experts. Our various boards and councils work on behalf of over 69,000 members in 59 countries—and in coordination with other influential public safety organizations—to ensure that the comprehensive system of emergency dispatching is safe, fast, effective and cutting-edge. IAED protocols are used in more than 4,080 communications centers across the world and have been time-tested across hundreds of millions of calls for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage dispatching. Learn more at <a href=\"https://www.emergencydispatch.org\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">www.emergencydispatch.org</a>.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-center wp-block-paragraph\">###</p>\n', 'Barbara Ireland of New Orleans, Louisiana Honored as  North America’s 911 Instructor of the Year', '<p>Award honors a 911 instructor for excellence in training and education</p>\n<p>April 18, 2023 (DENVER, Col.) — Barbara Ireland, Deputy Chief, retired, New Orleans EMS, is being honored today as North America’s 911 Instructor of the Year by the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAED), the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and the leading bo</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', 'a44026a1-f2fb-4bca-bb06-a4f63dc63759', '', '', '2023-04-18 15:12:00', '2023-04-18 15:12:00', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2023/04/18/a44026a1-f2fb-4bca-bb06-a4f63dc63759/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1215, 6, '2023-04-18 15:11:42', '2023-04-18 15:11:42', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><i><strong>April 18, 2023 (DENVER, Colo.) </strong></i>— More than 1,500 emergency response professionals are gathering in the Denver area from Tuesday through Thursday for the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch’s annual <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://navigator.emergencydispatch.org/home\"><span>NAVIGATOR</span></a> conference, the nation’s premier professional conference for medical, fire, and police dispatchers and secondary nurse triage providers.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Attendees will discuss a full range of hot topics including 911 workforce shortages, active shooters, electric car fires, callers experiencing mental health crises, and wellness for emergency responders.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">On Tuesday, the IAED is recognizing 87 call centers across the United States – including seven in Colorado – for earning the IAED Accredited Center of Excellence (ACE) credential. The ACE credential is based on completing a rigorous set of 20 Points of Accreditation in globally recognized best practices in emergency response.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The IAED also will bestow several prestigious awards, including North American 911 Dispatcher of the Year, North American 911 Instructor of the Year, and the Dr. Jeff J. Clawson Leadership Award.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The keynote presenter is U.S. Army veteran and <i>New York Times</i> best-selling author J.R. Martinez, who was severely burned when his Humvee was struck by a roadside bomb while on patrol in Iraq. Martinez’s will focus on ways to remain resilient and optimistic amid life’s most difficult situations.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Attendees also have the opportunity to tour the Denver Health Paramedics Center.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">As a U.S.-based non-profit with worldwide reach, the IAED is the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and is the leading body of emergency dispatch experts. Its boards and councils work on behalf of nearly 70,000 members in 59 countries — and in coordination with other influential public safety organizations — to ensure that the comprehensive system of emergency dispatching is safe, fast, effective, and cutting-edge. IAED protocols are used in more than 4,080 communications centers across the world and have been time-tested across hundreds of millions of calls for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage dispatching.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Learn more at <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.emergencydispatch.org\">www.emergencydispatch.org</a>.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-center wp-block-paragraph\">###</p>\n', 'More than 1,500 U.S. Medical, Fire, and Police Emergency Response Leaders Are Gathered in Denver for IAED’s 2023 NAVIGATOR Convention', '<p>Hot Topics Include Active Shooters, Mental Health, and 911 Staffing Strains</p>\n<p>April 18, 2023 (DENVER, Colo.) — More than 1,500 emergency response professionals are gathering in the Denver area from Tuesday through Thursday for the IAED&#8217;s annual NAVIGATOR conference, the nation’s premier professional conference for medical, fire, and police dispatchers and secondary nurse triage providers.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', 'acc055f6-bde1-4769-b0fa-35528add0bf9', '', '', '2023-04-18 15:11:42', '2023-04-18 15:11:42', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2023/04/18/acc055f6-bde1-4769-b0fa-35528add0bf9/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1216, 6, '2023-04-16 20:18:38', '2023-04-16 20:18:38', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">These 87 emergency dispatch centers representing medical, fire, police and emergency nurse triage responders have earned or re-earned IAED’s Accredited Center of Excellence (ACE) designation in 2023.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><a href=\"https://prioritydispatch.widen.net/s/q2v8vwg5hw/ace-and-re-ace-list-2023-delete\">Accredited or Re-Accredited ACEs 2023</a></p>\n', 'Newly Accredited or Re-Accredited ACEs, 2023', '<p>These 87 emergency dispatch centers representing medical, fire, police and emergency nurse triage responders have earned or re-earned the IAED’s Accredited Center of Excellence (ACE) designation in 2023.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', '8429ee86-cb0b-4350-8bf3-1932d7ab0fe2', '', '', '2023-04-16 20:18:38', '2023-04-16 20:18:38', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2023/04/16/8429ee86-cb0b-4350-8bf3-1932d7ab0fe2/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1217, 6, '2023-04-13 21:52:53', '2023-04-13 21:52:53', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">OnStar Emergency Advisors are Certified and Ready to Assist Callers with</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">IAED Police and Fire Protocols in Priority Dispatch’s ProQA Software</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Charlotte, NC, USA, and Oshawa, Ontario, Canada – Earlier this week, GM’s OnStar Emergency Advisors completed training and certification in emergency Police and Fire Protocols according to the exacting standards of the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAEDTM). This makes GM’s OnStar one of a select few private sector companies to receive certifications in fire, police and medical emergencies; equipping OnStar Emergency Advisors with tools to help OnStar members until first responders are on the scene.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In 2010, OnStar went live with Priority Dispatch’s calltaking software, ProQA® Paramount, and OnStar Emergency Advisors have been using the IAED Medical Protocols to gather crucial information to ensure first responders can be dispatched and work with callers through important pre-arrival steps and life-saving instructions for critical medical situations.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Now OnStar Emergency Advisors have specific Police Protocols to help callers through robberies, harassment, stalking, abduction — or even active shooter situations. Fire protocols mean OnStar Emergency Advisors are trained to give instructions for a backcountry rescue emergency, give survival instructions to a caller trapped in a vehicle in floodwater, or assist with instructions for a caller trapped in a structure fire.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“We’ve expanded OnStar support outside of the vehicle through the introduction of the OnStar Guardian app and the OnStar Guardian skill for Amazon Alexa,” said Mike Devereux, vice president of OnStar Safety and Connectivity at GM. “These new channels introduce new situations that will require the need for emergency police, fire, and medical assistance; and we want to make sure our OnStar Emergency Advisors are properly trained to support both our members and public safety agencies.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“OnStar Emergency Advisors are very well-trained,” said Pam Stewart, Senior Director of Operations for the IAED. “Two have even been recipients of the IAED Dispatcher of the Year (DOY) Award at our NAVIGATOR conference, which is happening next week in Denver. Last year at Nashville’s NAVIGATOR we recognized OnStar for recertifying as an Accredited Center of Excellence (ACE) in Medical, again. This is a tremendous achievement. They became an ACE in 2013 and have such a high level of dedication from leaders and calltakers that they’ve remained accredited ever since.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Brian Dale, President of Priority Dispatch, said, “Because it’s based on proven IAED protocols, ProQA software helps Advisors give their highest level of service every time they answer a call, and is even proven to help lower their anxiety. Our proactive, strengths-based Quality Management System helps OnStar assess the quality of care being provided and make positive adjustments to training and staff to meet their high standards.”</p>\n\n\n\n<h1 class=\"wp-block-heading\">#</h1>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">NAVIGATOR April 18-20, 2023 Denver, CO</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Dispatcher of the Year Details</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">2014: OnStar Emergency Advisor Bryan Anta</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Bryan took a call where the asthmatic on the other line was having an attack and couldn’t speak. He quickly thought to have the caller honk the horn once for yes, twice for no, to answer necessary questions. He instructed the caller to unlock the doors, and first responders arrived to help as she was passing in and out of consciousness.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">2016: OnStar Emergency Advisor Letisha Ghanbari</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Letisha took a call where a father was driving with his daughter in the backseat, ready to give birth, miles from help. Letisha was able to get the father to pull over and get in the backseat to help with the delivery. When he couldn’t find any kind of string or shoelace to tie off the umbilical cord, Letisha had him improvise with a phone charger cord for a successful delivery.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>About General Motors</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">General Motors (NYSE:GM) is a global company focused on advancing an all-electric future that is inclusive and accessible to all. At the heart of this strategy is the Ultium battery platform, which powers everything from mass-market to high-performance vehicles. General Motors, its subsidiaries and its joint venture entities sell vehicles under the Chevrolet, Buick, GMC, Cadillac, Baojun and Wuling brands. More information on the company and its subsidiaries, including OnStar, a global leader in vehicle safety and security services, can be found at <a href=\"https://www.gm.com\">https://www.gm.com</a>.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>About the IAED</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The IAED is the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and is the leading body of emergency dispatch experts. Our various boards and councils work on behalf of over 69,000 members in 59 countries—and in coordination with other influential public safety organizations—to ensure that the comprehensive system of emergency dispatching is safe, fast, effective, and cutting-edge. IAED protocols are used in more than 4,080 communications centers across the world and have been time-tested across hundreds of millions of calls for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage dispatching. Learn more at <a href=\"https://emergencydispatch.org\">emergencydispatch.org</a>.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>About Priority Dispatch</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Priority Dispatch is the world leader in providing research-based protocol solutions to emergency call centers in medical, fire, police, and nurse triage disciplines. For over 40 years, EMS and 911 agencies have used the IAED-approved Medical Priority Dispatch System TM (MPDS), first with cardsets and now in ProQA® Paramount software. Our solutions include structured protocols and a gold-standard Quality Management Program, as well as training, certification, and continuing education—all time-tested across hundreds of millions of calls and serving communities in thousands of agencies around the world. Now in 30 languages and 59 countries. Get to know us at <a href=\"https://prioritydispatch.net\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">prioritydispatch.net</a>.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"></p>\n', 'OnStar Emergency Advisors are Certified and Ready to Assist Callers with  IAED Police and Fire Protocols in Priority Dispatch’s ProQA Software', '<p>GM’s OnStar Emergency Advisors completed training and certification in emergency Police and Fire Protocols according to the exacting standards of the IAED, making them one of a select few private sector companies to use all three Police, Fire, and Medical Protocols; equipping Advisors with the industry&#8217;s top tools to help OnStar members until first responders are on scene.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', 'e1d1ff26-79b9-4e20-aec1-1cfc4eb4a1e5', '', '', '2023-04-13 21:52:53', '2023-04-13 21:52:53', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2023/04/13/e1d1ff26-79b9-4e20-aec1-1cfc4eb4a1e5/', 0, 'post', '', 0);
INSERT INTO `wp_posts` (`ID`, `post_author`, `post_date`, `post_date_gmt`, `post_content`, `post_title`, `post_excerpt`, `post_status`, `comment_status`, `ping_status`, `post_password`, `post_name`, `to_ping`, `pinged`, `post_modified`, `post_modified_gmt`, `post_content_filtered`, `post_parent`, `guid`, `menu_order`, `post_type`, `post_mime_type`, `comment_count`) VALUES
(1218, 6, '2023-02-28 04:59:12', '2023-02-28 04:59:12', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong><em>Increasing call volumes and severe staffing shortages strain 911 services in over half of U.S. states, according to an IAED/NASNA Study</em></strong></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>February 28, 2023 (SALT LAKE CITY, UT) — </strong>As the volume of emergency calls continues to rise year by year, adequate staffing is one of the most significant issues facing nearly half of all emergency call centers across the country, according to a new study jointly conducted by the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch<sup>®</sup> (IAED<sup>™</sup>) and the National Association of State 911 Administrators (NASNA).</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The study is being released as hundreds of 911 leaders descend on Washington, D.C. this week for “9-1-1 Goes to Washington,” an annual <a href=\"https://www.nena.org/page/gtw\" target=\"_blank\">advocacy and education event</a> convened by NENA: The 9-1-1 Association.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The survey was responded to by 774 911 centers in 47 states across the United States. Among the key findings:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>While 27% of centers reported no change in actual staffing levels over the most recent four-year period, 36% reported having fewer positions filled in 2022 than they did in 2019.</li>\n\n\n\n<li>The 911 staffing problem appears to be wide ranging, affecting parts of every state and every sized 911 center. The 25 centers reporting the greatest losses are located in all regions of the country, and the average vacancy rate is similar across all sizes of agencies.</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Nearly one-third reported stunning vacancy rates in 2022. Thirteen reported that 70% or more of positions were unfilled; 92 reported a vacancy rate between 50-69%; and 166 reported rates of 30% to 49%.</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“An alarming number of 911 centers are experiencing their own workforce emergencies,” said Harriet Rennie-Brown, Executive Director of NASNA. “Better workforce recruitment, retention, and support are crucial to the quality of the 911 emergency services we all rely on.” &nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“When 911 is contacted to assist in an emergency, both time and skilled dispatchers are of the essence. But when there are more incoming demands on the 911 center than there are trained, qualified personnel to respond, public safety is put at risk,” said Ty Wooten, IAED Director of Government Affairs.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Nearly every respondent reported a loss of employees in 2022, for a total of 3,952 staff departures across the 774 centers in one year. Telecommunicators and emergency dispatchers with at least one year of experience reported leaving the profession due to the stress of the job, or for better work hours, better opportunities, or better pay.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Employees cited pay increases and better benefits as the top two retention incentives, although agencies are struggling to meet this demand in areas where public safety budgets are already being squeezed.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Several 911 leaders also noted the importance of state and federal reclassification of 911 professionals as a “protective service” occupation, as called for by the federal 911 SAVES Act, which would help ensure that emergency dispatchers receive the respect and support other first responders receive.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For more detailed information from the report, download the <a href=\"https://prioritydispatch.widen.net/content/zgxckrz7ij/original/IAED_NASNA-Survey-Data-Sheet.pdf?u=7cbckq&amp;download=true\" target=\"_blank\">IAED-NASNA Workforce Survey Data Sheet</a>. Subject matter experts are available for interviews.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>About the IAED</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The IAED is the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and is the leading body of emergency dispatch experts. Our various boards and councils work on behalf of over 69,000 members in 59 countries—and in coordination with other influential public safety organizations—to ensure that the comprehensive system of emergency dispatching is safe, fast, effective and cutting-edge. IAED protocols are used in more than 4,080 communications centers across the world and have been time-tested across hundreds of millions of calls for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage dispatching. Learn more at <a href=\"https://www.emergencydispatch.org\" target=\"_blank\">EmergencyDispatch.org</a>.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>About NASNA</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The National Association of State 911 Administrators (NASNA) is the voice of the states on public policy issues impacting 911. NASNA focuses on three areas: developing strategic partnerships with key organizations and individuals who share our interests; serving as an information sharing and support network for state 911 program administrators; and strengthening relationships with federal lawmakers and agencies. Learn more at <a href=\"https://www.nasna911.org/home\" target=\"_blank\">nasna911.org</a></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Download the entire press release at the link below.</p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image is-resized\"><a href=\"https://prioritydispatch.widen.net/content/u8mwjflaug/original/News-Release-Emergency-Workforce-Survey-20220228.pdf?u=7cbckq&amp;download=true\" target=\"_blank\"><img decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://prioritydispatch.widen.net/content/u8mwjflaug/jpeg/News-Release-Emergency-Workforce-Survey-20220228.jpg?u=7cbckq&amp;w=200\" alt=\"\"/></a></figure>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Download the <a href=\"https://prioritydispatch.widen.net/content/zgxckrz7ij/original/IAED_NASNA-Survey-Data-Sheet.pdf?u=7cbckq&amp;download=true\" target=\"_blank\">IAED-NASNA Workforce Survey Data Sheet</a></p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image is-resized\"><a href=\"https://prioritydispatch.widen.net/content/zgxckrz7ij/original/IAED_NASNA-Survey-Data-Sheet.pdf?u=7cbckq&amp;download=true\" target=\"_blank\"><img decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://prioritydispatch.widen.net/content/zgxckrz7ij/jpeg/IAED_NASNA-Survey-Data-Sheet.jpg?u=7cbckq&amp;w=200\" alt=\"\"/></a></figure>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"></p>\n', 'More Than Half of U.S. 911 Centers Are Facing Their Own Emergency', '<p>Increasing call volumes and severe staffing shortages strain 911 services in over half of U.S. states, according to an IAED/NASNA Study.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', '64632133-7f9f-4d77-8013-d59c445fdb88', '', '', '2023-02-28 04:59:12', '2023-02-28 04:59:12', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2023/02/28/64632133-7f9f-4d77-8013-d59c445fdb88/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1219, 6, '2023-02-28 03:31:15', '2023-02-28 03:31:15', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">IAED™ and NASNA co-sponsored a survey of 774 911 Centers to better understand the workforce challenges—and opportunities—facing their agencies today. While some 911 centers surveyed report being fully staffed,&nbsp;others report staggering vacancy rates of up to 83%.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><a href=\"https://prioritydispatch.widen.net/s/nhjnnm5dbf/iaed_nasna-survey-data-sheet\">Download the IAED &#8211; NASNA Survey Data Sheet</a></p>\n', 'IN THEIR WORDS&#8230;Leaders of 911 Centers Across America Share Their Views on the Workforce Challenge', '<p>IAED™ and NASNA co-sponsored a survey of 774 911 Centers to better understand the workforce challenges—and opportunities—facing their agencies today. While some 911 centers surveyed report being fully staffed,<br />\nothers report staggering vacancy rates of up to 83%.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', '884dcad0-1342-4c8f-8519-a60f8236079c', '', '', '2023-02-28 03:31:15', '2023-02-28 03:31:15', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2023/02/28/884dcad0-1342-4c8f-8519-a60f8236079c/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1220, 6, '2021-05-25 00:00:00', '2021-05-25 00:00:00', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><em>His Goal: Impact Rules and Regulations of Emergency Communications Profession—Ty Wooten Signs on as New IAED Director of Governmental Affairs.</em></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Salt Lake City, UT (May 21, 2021) — He’s done it all in what he calls a “No failure game.” Ty Wooten has been in emergency communications for nearly 27 years as a firefighter, EMT, hostage negotiator, dispatcher, Director of the Hancock County (Indiana) 911 Center, and more. Now, he’s joined the International Academies of Emergency Dispatch (IAED) as the Director of Governmental Affairs.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“We are beyond thrilled and honored to have Ty at IAED. His skill and expertise are practically legendary in the industry. He brings a unique set of experiences and knowledge that we believe can have an impact on the rules, regulations, and advocacy of the profession,” commented Pam Stewart, IAED Director of Operations and Board of Certification Chair. “And he’s a true believer in the importance of scripted Protocol.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Wooten, who also served as the Director of the Indianapolis International Airport Communications Center, commends IAED and the many experts in the field who are behind the science and research of the Protocol—and who are forever committed to honing the process.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“What we do, what the Protocol does, has such an impact on people’s lives. There’s no room for failure. People’s lives depend on this. Every single life we touch matters. No exception. No matter who calls or who answers, it’s got to be done right,” states Wooten.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Most recently the Director of Education for the National Emergency Number Association (NENA), Wooten says that the Protocol makes the process simple, ensuring emergency dispatchers do it right every single time for every single caller. But he maintains that, “there’s still a lot the emergency dispatcher brings to the call, like customer service for the person in crisis on the other end of the line, and for the responders we send to the scene—it all plays a huge role in 911 effectiveness.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">As an advocate for the people and the profession, Wooten believes in the importance of pushing the United States Office of Management and Budget (OMB) to change the classification of emergency dispatchers to be more aligned with other emergency service professionals.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“We are building the argument, showing the OMB the magnitude of requirements. It’s about asking the right questions, sending the appropriate response, and giving the life-saving instructions,” he concludes.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For more information about the IAED, visit www.emergencydispatch.org.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">About the IAED The IAED is the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and is the leading body of emergency dispatch experts. Our various boards and councils work on behalf of over 69,000 members in 54 countries—and in coordination with other influential public safety organizations—to ensure that the comprehensive system of emergency dispatching is safe, fast, effective, and cutting-edge. IAED protocols are used in more than 3,800 communications centers across the world and have been time-tested across hundreds of millions of calls for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage dispatching.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"mailto:?subject=IAED%20Article&amp;body=Check%20out%20this%20IAED%20article:%20https://emergencydistg.wpenginepowered.com/news/press-releases/b7f9c993-ae91-4cda-863b-a8cac0faeb80\"></a><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.facebook.com/sharer/sharer.php?u=https://emergencydistg.wpenginepowered.com/news/press-releases/b7f9c993-ae91-4cda-863b-a8cac0faeb80\"></a><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?text=Check%20out%20this%20IAED%20article:%20https://emergencydistg.wpenginepowered.com/news/press-releases/b7f9c993-ae91-4cda-863b-a8cac0faeb80\"></a><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/shareArticle?url=https://emergencydistg.wpenginepowered.com/news/press-releases/b7f9c993-ae91-4cda-863b-a8cac0faeb80\"></a></p>\n', 'Ty Wooten Signs on as New IAED Director of Governmental Affairs', '<p>He’s done it all in what he calls a “No failure game.” Ty Wooten has been in emergency communications for nearly 27 years as a firefighter, EMT, hostage negotiator, dispatcher, Director of the Hancock County (Indiana) 911 Center, and more. Now, he’s joined the</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', 'b7f9c993-ae91-4cda-863b-a8cac0faeb80', '', '', '2021-05-25 00:00:00', '2021-05-25 00:00:00', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2021/05/25/b7f9c993-ae91-4cda-863b-a8cac0faeb80/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1221, 6, '2021-04-29 00:00:00', '2021-04-29 00:00:00', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Salt Lake City, UT (April 29, 2021) – The <a href=\"https://emergencydistg.wpenginepowered.com/who-we-are#iaed-leadership\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">International Academies of Emergency Dispatch (IAED)</a> is pleased to announce its appointment of Ian Adams, Ph.D. candidate, 2018 American Society of Public Administration Founders’ Fellow, and 2020 Doctoral Fellow of the Academy of Criminal Justice Sciences, as Chair of Board of Trustees. “Our Board of Trustees is comprised of professionals who are dedicated to the advancement of emergency dispatch science,” said Pam Stewart, IAED Director of Operations and Board of Certification Chair. “As a cross-disciplinary scholar in public administration, policing policy, labor law, and criminal justice, Ian’s expertise, vision, and commitment will be a great contribution in helping us further our mission to advance and support the public-safety emergency telecommunications professional.” </p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“As a practitioner and researcher who focuses on the experiences of front-line law enforcement employees, both civilian and sworn, I have been honored to contribute to the IAED as a member of the Board of Trustees and am excited for the opportunity to continue my service there as incoming Chair of Board of Trustees,” says Adams. In addition to the lengthy list of contributions he has made to academia, Adams has served as Executive Director for the Utah Fraternal Order of Police since 2016. In that role, he represents the voice of nearly 4,500 law enforcement officers in legal, political, and media environments. His approach to leadership is based on over twelve years of experience as a law enforcement officer, and his research interests include body-worn cameras in law enforcement, increasing recruitment of women in law enforcement, and mental health harm reduction in first responders. “The reason why I was interested in serving on the board is because the role of dispatch in police operations is incredibly critical, and I want to ensure it gets the support and adoption it deserves from the police community.” Adams says that as he became more educated in policy and policing, and delved into his own academic research, he began to see the value in aggregating large amounts of data to develop the best scientific practices possible, which is also IAED’s focus—to gather data and research to improve dispatch. “I’m here to support the mission of IAED and aim to use my scientific research to inform and build policies that improve law enforcement for its employees and the communities that depend on them. </p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">We cannot hope to improve the system while ignoring the voices of those within the profession, and so I seek to elevate the concerns and experiences of front-line employees—especially during today’s unsettled police operations.” For more information about the IAED, visit <a href=\"https://www.emergencydispatch.org\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">www.emergencydispatch.org</a>. About the IAED The IAED is the standard-setting organization for emergency dispatch and response services worldwide and is the leading body of emergency dispatch experts. Our various boards and councils work on behalf of over 69,000 members in 54 countries—and in coordination with other influential public safety organizations—to ensure that the comprehensive system of emergency dispatching is safe, fast, effective, and cutting-edge. IAED protocols are used in more than 3,800 communications centers across the world and have been time-tested across hundreds of millions of calls for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage dispatching.</p>\n', 'New IAED Chair of Board of Trustees Believes Role of Dispatch in Police Operations Critical', '<p>Salt Lake City, UT (April 29, 2021) – The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch (IAED) is pleased to announce its appointment of Ian Adams, Ph.D. candidate, 2018 American Society of Public Administration Founders’ Fellow, and 2020 Doctoral Fellow of th</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', '51b75dd3-1112-4c24-8c0f-220975f8bf76', '', '', '2021-04-29 00:00:00', '2021-04-29 00:00:00', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2021/04/29/51b75dd3-1112-4c24-8c0f-220975f8bf76/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1222, 6, '2020-12-08 00:00:00', '2020-12-08 00:00:00', '<p>The Dispatcher of the Year Award is one of the most distinctive awards presented by IAED and was created to identify and recognize individuals who have made the most significant contributions throughout the year to further the values of IAED and emergency dispatch&#8230;</p>\n<div class=\"no-print\"><a class=\"px-button news-pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\"><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://prioritydispatch.widen.net/content/0cfkqpuflz/original/Australasia-DOY-2020-Award_Press-Release.pdf?u=7cbckq\">Read More&nbsp;</a></a></div>\n', 'IAED HONOURS DISPATCHER OF THE YEAR RECIPIENT FOR 2020 AUSTRALASIA NAVIGATOR', '<p>The Dispatcher of the Year Award is one of the most distinctive awards presented by IAED and was created to identify and recognize individuals who have made the most significant contributions throughout the year to further the values of IAED and emergency</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', '40ecbd47-1677-49a8-b11a-78068041e069', '', '', '2020-12-08 00:00:00', '2020-12-08 00:00:00', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2020/12/08/40ecbd47-1677-49a8-b11a-78068041e069/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1223, 6, '2020-10-28 00:00:00', '2020-10-28 00:00:00', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The Dispatcher of the Year Award is one of the most distinctive awards presented by IAED and was created to identify and recognize individuals who have made the most significant contributions throughout the year to further the values of IAED and emergency dispatch&#8230;</p>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"no-print\"><a class=\"px-button news-pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\"></a><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://prioritydispatch.widen.net/content/8qslfjflmt/original/EUROPEAN-Dispatcher-of-the-Year.pdf?u=7cbckq\">Read More&nbsp;</a></div>\n', 'IAED HONOURS DISPATCHER OF THE YEAR RECIPIENTS FOR 2020 EURO &#038; UK NAVIGATOR', '<p>The Dispatcher of the Year Award is one of the most distinctive awards presented by IAED and was created to identify and recognize individuals who have made the most significant contributions throughout the year to further the values of IAED and emergency disp</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', 'b17a9365-b546-41d2-8a8a-7bdf9b8deb57', '', '', '2020-10-28 00:00:00', '2020-10-28 00:00:00', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2020/10/28/b17a9365-b546-41d2-8a8a-7bdf9b8deb57/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1224, 6, '2020-05-28 00:00:00', '2020-05-28 00:00:00', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAED™ ) is pleased to announce the COVID-19 Special Release #2 with **Medical Priority Dispatch System™ v13.3.122 (5/27/2020) and MPDS® v13.2.251 (5/21/2020)** as part of ProQA v5.1.1.36&#8230;</p>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"no-print\">\n  <a class=\"px-button news-pdf\" href=\"https://myemail.constantcontact.com/Announcing-COVID-19-Special-Release--2.html?soid=1130951738435&amp;aid=bEH0opsJm5M\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\"><br>\n  Read More<br>\n  </a>\n</div>\n', 'Special COVID-19 Release #2 with MPDS v13.3.122 (5/27/2020)', '<p>The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAED™ ) is pleased to announce the COVID-19 Special Release #2 with Medical Priority Dispatch System™ v13.3.122 (5/27/2020) and MPDS® v13.2.251 (5/21/2020) as part of ProQA v5.1.1.36&#8230;</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', '1b28b857-bcfc-4943-960e-6891915b35fa', '', '', '2020-05-28 00:00:00', '2020-05-28 00:00:00', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2020/05/28/1b28b857-bcfc-4943-960e-6891915b35fa/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1225, 6, '2020-03-30 00:00:00', '2020-03-30 00:00:00', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">This <strong>COVID-19 Special Release</strong> is to support you and your communities around the world in this critical and unprecedented time when your resources and systems are strained. We&#8217;ve outlined key elements in MPDS® that have been added and upgraded, and in both FPDS® and PPDS®, the new COVID-19-Specific EIDS Tool has been added to identify symptoms related to COVID-19.</p>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"no-print\">\n  <a class=\"px-button news-pdf\" href=\"https://conta.cc/3ddfzOy\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\"><br>\n  Read More<br>\n  </a>\n</div>\n', 'IAED Announces Special COVID-19 Release with MPDS v13.3.97 (3/30/2020)', '<p>This COVID-19 Special Release is to support you and your communities around the world in this critical and unprecedented time when your resources and systems are strained&#8230;</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', 'f039d4c3-4c37-4bd8-997b-5881baff553a', '', '', '2020-03-30 00:00:00', '2020-03-30 00:00:00', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2020/03/30/f039d4c3-4c37-4bd8-997b-5881baff553a/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1226, 6, '2018-10-23 00:00:00', '2018-10-23 00:00:00', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAED™) Fire Council of Standards has approved the release of an updated version of the Fire Priority Dispatch System™ (FPDS®). FPDS version 7.0 includes significant changes and new additions in an effort to help emergency dispatchers send a faster, more informed response.  </p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In what is the largest FPDS release to date, this new version implements revisions from more than 200 proposals from change. Version 7.0 includes adjustments to existing Pre-Arrival Instructions (PAIs) and Post-Dispatch Instruction (PDIs), which will more effectively assist emergency dispatchers as they guide callers through lifesaving actions while waiting for firefighters and other emergency personnel to arrive on the scene. </p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The IAED is also excited to announce that version 7.0 also includes six new protocols, which will, when necessary, place the emergency dispatcher in a role as a rescuer. These additional protocols are:<br>\n&#8211; 79: Backcountry Rescue<br>\n&#8211; 79: Lost Person<br>\n&#8211; 80: Outside Tank Fire<br>\n&#8211; 81: Sinking Vehicle/Vehicle in Floodwater<br>\n&#8211; 82: Vegetation/Wildland/Brush/Grass Fire<br>\n&#8211; 83: Weather/Disaster Situations </p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The decision to make these significant changes largely came from an increase in catastrophic events with record numbers of callers requesting assistance. In recent years, there has been an escalation in deaths from fires and these related incidents. The IAED saw a crucial opportunity to create these new protocols and make changes to current ones. </p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Data showed that six new rescue scenarios would be beneficial and that these would help the emergency dispatcher send a more informed response earlier in the questioning sequence. Catastrophic event data also showed that emergency resources were quickly depleted, delaying first responder arrivals or even making them impossible. Acting on further data, the IAED decided that FPDS v7.0 needed to move away from the prevailing “shelter in place until the firefighters arrive” position and provide life-saving instructions in instances where death is imminent. </p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“One of the most notable new features of the FPDS v7.0 is a shift in philosophy with respect to intervention in caller/bystander safety with Dispatch Life Support Instructions,” said Mike Thompson, IAED Fire Council of Standards Chair. “In past versions, a primarily passive approach was taken to some caller actions. In many situations, the default instruction was to do nothing and wait for the Fire Department to arrive. The IAED Fire Council of Standards and the FPDS in some immediately or potentially immediately life-threatening situations is now taking a much more dynamic approach.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Priority Dispatch® has also updated the protocols in its ProQA® software to reflect these FPDS v7.0 changes. </p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“At the Academy and Priority Dispatch, our goal is to help the emergency dispatcher do his or her job better,” said Dr. Jeff Clawson, Chair, Rules Committee for the IAED Medical Council of Standards. “This increases safety and effectiveness for the first responders and creates better outcomes for callers. By implementing the IAED’s FPDS v7.0 changes in ProQA, we know that certified emergency dispatchers using ProQA have the best and most intelligent tool available to reliably and efficiently help their callers and first responders.” </p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For more information about FPDS v7.0 and the IAED, visit the website at [emergencydispatch.org](/home). For more information about how the Medical Priority Dispatch System can increase the effectiveness and safety of your EMS system, visit <a href=\"https://www.prioritydispatch.net\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">prioritydispatch.net</a>.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">#### About the IAED</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The IAED is the world’s foremost standard-setting and certification organization for emergency communications with over 64,000 members in 46 countries. More than 3,000 communication centers in 23 languages employ IAED’s protocols and training in medical, fire, and police dispatching.</p>\n', 'International Academies of Emergency Dispatch Announces the Release of FPDS v7.0 (10/23/2018)', '<p>The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAED™) Fire Council of Standards has approved Fire Priority Dispatch System™ v7.0 (FPDS®), which includes significant changes and additions to help emergency dispatchers send a faster, more informed response.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', '7f9806d2-dd77-44a2-92fc-974d3d69d196', '', '', '2018-10-23 00:00:00', '2018-10-23 00:00:00', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2018/10/23/7f9806d2-dd77-44a2-92fc-974d3d69d196/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1227, 6, '2018-05-02 00:00:00', '2018-05-02 00:00:00', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAED™) ED-Q Council of Standards has approved the release of Performance Standards 10th Edition, an updated version of performance requirements for all medical, fire, and police emergency calltaking. These new standards are integrated seamlessly into AQUA® 7 Ascent and will enhance a communication center&#8217;s ability to review cases and manage the quality assurance (QA) process. Standards 10 is the result of more than two years of feedback and input from members across the world desiring a more positive approach to quality review. While in the past communication center QA professionals, called ED-Qs, have used the Performance Standards to identify deviations from protocol, Standards 10 helps management assess overall calltaking performance of emergency dispatchers. </p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">By using Standards 10, managers can employ a more positive approach to quality assurance by delivering feedback using a strengths-based approach. Brian Dale, Associate Director of Medical Control and Quality Processes for the IAED, said, &#8220;Standards 10 makes it possible for management, ED-Qs, and emergency dispatchers to focus on improving the system, both of the calltakers and the software they&#8217;re using. Our feedback at NAVIGATOR was very positive, and we&#8217;re excited for everyone to begin using Standards 10.&#8221; ED-Qs will benefit from the shorter, more succinct Standards that enable calls to be reviewed more efficiently, quickly, and accurately. Due to new research, these standards reduce call review requirements in most agencies, which ED-Qs will also appreciate. Emergency dispatchers will also welcome the introduction of Standards 10. Previous versions required ED-Qs to emphasize minor emergency calltaking mistakes, often leading to low morale in the communication center where emergency dispatchers are already tasked with a high-pressure, stressful job. In some cases, the review process could strain relationships between emergency dispatchers and management. </p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Standards 10 will help balance these negative feelings by also acknowledging the positive performance and excellent work of emergency dispatchers. For more information about Standards 10 and the IAED, visit <a href=\"https://emergencydistg.wpenginepowered.com/what-we-do/performance-standards\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" class=\"inline-link\">https://emergencydistg.wpenginepowered.com/what-we-do/performance-standards</a>. For more information about how AQUA 7 Ascent can improve your center&#8217;s review process, visit <a href=\"https://prioritydispatch.net/aqua/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">www.prioritydispatch.net/aqua</a>. </p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>About the IAED</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The IAED is the world&#8217;s foremost standard-setting and certification organization for emergency communications with over 64,000 members in 46 countries. More than 3,600 communication centers in 23 languages employ IAED&#8217;s protocols and training in medical, fire, police, and nurse triage dispatching.</p>\n', 'IAED Releases Performance Standards 10th edition', '<p>The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAED™) ED-Q Council<br />\nof Standards has approved the release of Performance Standards 10th<br />\nEdition, an updated version of performance requirements for all medical,<br />\nfire, and police&#8230;</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', 'ecb5792c-25ba-4386-aecc-d794c3aec2c6', '', '', '2018-05-02 00:00:00', '2018-05-02 00:00:00', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2018/05/02/ecb5792c-25ba-4386-aecc-d794c3aec2c6/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1228, 6, '2017-02-27 00:00:00', '2017-02-27 00:00:00', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAED™) is pleased to announce that Brett Patterson, IAED Chair of the Medical Council of Standards and Academics &amp; Standards Associate, received the Journal of Emergency Medical Services’  EMS10: Innovators in EMS Award Feb. 22 at JEMS annual EMS Today Conference &amp; Exposition, held this year for the first time in Salt Lake City, Utah.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The JEMS accolade recognizes 10 recipients who demonstrate their innovation in moving the level of EMS care forward during 2016. </p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“Brett becomes one of an elite group of people with only 10 being selected each year for this prestigious award,” said A.J. Heightman, JEMS Editor-in-Chief. “It&#8217;s not only a prestigious award but it also gives a window into the world of EMS. And because of his work and the other nine selectees’ contributions, we&#8217;re able to progress and move EMS to the next level. Being an innovator means developing ways that are more efficient and better for patient care, and Brett exemplifies that.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Patterson directed the Academy’s construction and debut of the Medical Priority Dispatch System™ (MPDS®) version 13.0 with which he was personally involved in reviewing of all Proposals for Change (PFCs), directing protocol research, and leading the Council through months of evaluation and protocol development. His numerous Academy roles include protocol standards and evolution, cultural and language development, research, curriculum, training, and quality improvement. A member of the IAED College of Fellows and Rules Committee, Patterson became a paramedic in 1981 and began his EMS communications career in 1987. Prior to joining the Academy, Patterson served for 10 years as an EMS professional for Pinellas County, Florida, USA.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“The recent scientific realization that basic life support quality is perhaps the most important link in the sudden cardiac arrest chain of survival has put a spotlight on the critical role dispatchers play in resuscitation,” Patterson said. “I have been honored to play a small part in helping dispatchers to understand and appreciate this role by helping to evolve the clinical standard of dispatch care via protocol.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The IAED is the world’s foremost standard-setting, certification-granting organization for emergency communications with over 62,000 members in 45 countries. More than 3,000 communication centers in 21 languages and dialects employ IAED’s protocols and training in medical, fire, police, and emergency nurse triage dispatching. Of those, nearly 200 comm. centers have become Accredited Centers of Excellence (ACEs).</p>\n', 'IAED’S Brett Patterson Lauded as JEMS’ EMS10 Innovator at EMS Today', '<p>SALT LAKE CITY—The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAED™) is pleased to announce that Brett Patterson, IAED Chair of the Medical Council of Standards and Academics &#038; Standards Associate, received the Journal of&#8230;</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', '94d4b3b6-8da2-452c-bf11-11211743d383', '', '', '2017-02-27 00:00:00', '2017-02-27 00:00:00', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2017/02/27/94d4b3b6-8da2-452c-bf11-11211743d383/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1229, 6, '2016-06-08 00:00:00', '2016-06-08 00:00:00', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>INDIANAPOLIS, Ind. (June 9, 2016)</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A group of 9-1-1 organizations jointly will announce, during the annual National Emergency Number Association (NENA) trade show and conference, that it finalized recommended minimum training guidelines for the nation’s 9-1-1 call-takers and dispatchers, culminating a three-year effort. The topics to be included in the recommended guidelines were identified by members of the following organizations, as well as other key stakeholders representing the 9-1-1 community, including: &#8211; Association of Public-Safety Communications Officials (APCO) &#8211; Connecticut Department of Emergency Services &amp; Public Protection &#8211; Denise Amber Lee Foundation &#8211; Fairfax County (Virginia) Department of Public Safety &#8211; Greater Harris County 9-1-1 Emergency Network &#8211; Hamilton County (Indiana) Public Safety Communications &#8211; International Academies of Emergency Dispatch (IAED) &#8211; National Association of State 9-1-1 Administrators (NASNA) &#8211; National Emergency Number Association (NENA) &#8211; National Fire Protection Association (NFPA) &#8211; North Central Texas Council of Governments (NCTCOG) &#8211; PowerPhone &#8211; Priority Dispatch &#8211; RedSky Technologies &#8211; Rochester/Monroe County (New York) Emergency Communications Department &#8211; Stafford County (Virginia) Sheriff’s Office &#8211; Vail (Colorado) Public Safety Communications Center &#8211; Weakley County (Tennessee) Emergency 9-1-1 The effort was driven by the belief that it is vitally important Americans receive a consistent level of 9-1-1 service no matter where they live or travel, said Nathan Lee, president of the Denise Amber Lee Foundation. “Consequently, there must be agreed-upon common elements that ensure the person who answers a 9-1-1 call has met baseline core competencies,” Lee said. “We believe that the result of this effort will be that the public ultimately will receive a more consistent, effective and professional level of service from telecommunicators across the United States.” </p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">While the National 9-1-1 Program has facilitated the Project by providing a forum for discussion and development, the Guidelines are not federally owned or mandated—rather, they are the joint product of members of the Working Group listed above. The recommended minimum training guidelines cover the following topics: &#8211; Telecommunicator roles and responsibilities &#8211; 9-1-1 call processing &#8211; Radio communications &#8211; Emergency management &#8211; Emergency communications technology &#8211; Legal concepts &#8211; Interpersonal communications &#8211; Stress management &#8211; Quality assurance &#8211; On-the-job training guidelines In addition, numerous subtopics were identified for each topic listed above. It should be noted that the topics identified in the Guidelines provide minimum-level understanding. In order to field and manage emergency calls in a live environment, telecommunicators must receive supplemental training that will enable them to process the discipline-specific emergency calls that are fielded by their respective PSAP/9-1-1 Center or Emergency Services Provider. The Guidelines were vetted by the 9-1-1 community at large, via the NENA Workspace platform, before they were finalized. </p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">All comments received were adjudicated by a subcommittee formed specifically for this purpose; the subcommittee was comprised of Working Group members who had volunteered for the task. “The comments received were positive and supportive of the effort, and the result,” said Laurie Flaherty, program manager for the National 911 Program. “This is a watershed moment for the 9-1-1 community, and many people put their heart and soul into making these guidelines a reality.” A parallel goal of the Project was to develop Model Legislation for any state that does not currently have legislation concerning minimum training for telecommunicators. For those that do, the Model Legislation is intended as a baseline to ensure that the recommended minimum training topics are being covered. </p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The Academy’s Emergency Telecommunicator Course (ETC) was developed in the late 1990s and is kept current by the ETC Board of Curriculum. Along with on-the-job training, the ETC course meets and exceeds the recommended minimum training guidelines for all 9-1-1 telecommunicators as redefined in this needed, nationwide framework for the baseline of training required for all 911 calltakers and dispatchers – regardless of the discipline(s) they work in. When ETC education is combined with the structured call-taking protocols, continuing education training,and quality improvement programs found in the Academy’s Emergency Medical, Fire, Police, and NurseTriage courses, telecommunicators become fully trained and prepared for today’s challenging emergency dispatch environment.</p>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"no-print\"><a class=\"px-button news-pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\"></a><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://prioritydispatch.widen.net/content/nb7jhnssns/original/Training-Guidelines-Information_v10-2.pdf?u=7cbckq\">Download Original&nbsp;</a></div>\n', 'Recommended Minimum Training Guidelines For Telecommunicators Finalized', '<p>INDIANAPOLIS, Ind. (June 9, 2016)—A group of 9-1-1 organizations jointly will announce, during the annual National Emergency Number Association (NENA) trade show and conference, that it finalized recommended minimum training guidelines for&#8230;</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', '2abdc765-096e-4376-9638-47ec388b9a9b', '', '', '2016-06-08 00:00:00', '2016-06-08 00:00:00', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2016/06/08/2abdc765-096e-4376-9638-47ec388b9a9b/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1230, 6, '2016-06-03 00:00:00', '2016-06-03 00:00:00', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">SALT LAKE CITY—The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAED™) has released an updated version of its Fire Priority Dispatch System™ (FPDS®) v6.1 that more accurately differentiates two incident types covered under Protocols 60, 61, and 66.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">FPDS v6.1a seeks to make the distinction between the two incident types—the chief complaint’s unknown odor and natural gas leak—clearer by removing the rotten egg description of a suspected natural gas leak from the Protocol 61: HAZMAT shunt on Protocol 66: Odor (Strange/Unknown). While using the FPDS, the foremost chief complaint of a “rotten eggs or sulfur” odor should be handled on Protocol 60: Gas Leak/Gas Odor (Natural and LP Gases). As a safety net, Protocol 66 shunts the calltaker to Protocol 60 to which the clarifier “(rotten eggs/sulfur)” has been added.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For chemical suicide, the foremost chief complaint is usually an unresponsive person in a vehicle or small confined space—such as a closet or bathroom—with warning signs taped up. The odor of rotten eggs or sulfur may also be present; however, the odor is secondary to the foremost chief complaint of a chemical suicide.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Formerly, Protocol 66 had a gray shunt for “Rotten eggs/Sulfur (HAZMAT)” to Protocol 61, but this shunt has been modified in FPDS v6.1a to remove the description of “Rotten eggs/Sulfur,” and now appears as “Chemical/HAZMAT” to prevent incorrectly shunting to Protocol 61. Two new rules have also been added to Protocol 66 to aid calltakers in choosing the most appropriate protocol.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“In the past year in particular, we have had a number of cases sent to us in which the stated chief complaint was ‘smell of rotten eggs,’ said Mike Thompson, Academy Fire Curriculum Council Chair and Priority Dispatch System™ Program Administrator—Fire &amp; Medical. “Of those cases that were sent to us, almost exclusively they turned out to be natural gas leaks that ultimately were handled on Protocol 61 because of the shunt added to Protocol 66 to capture chemical suicide cases. But Protocol 61 was not set up to handle the inherent explosion risk and scene safety issues that come with a natural gas leak case, and so it needed to be corrected.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">FPDS v6.0 and v6.1 users will receive automatic FPDS v6.1a upgrades for Priority Dispatch Corp.’s™ ProQA® calltaking software and/or updated Protocol cards for their cardsets. For more information about FPDS v6.1a, visit <a href=\"/\" class=\"inline-link\"><br> www.emergencydispatch.org</a>.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>About the IAED</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Founded in 1988, the IAED is the foremost standard-setting and certification organization for emergency communications with more than 59,500 members in 45 countries. More than 3,650 communication centers take advantage of IAED’s protocols and training—translated into 22 languages/dialects—for medical, fire, police, and nurse triage dispatch.</p>\n', 'Academy Upgrades Fire Protocol’s Natural Gas-Related Protocols With FPDS v6.1a', '<p>The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAED™) has released an updated version of its Fire Priority Dispatch System™ (FPDS®) v6.1 that more accurately differentiates two incident types covered under Protocols 60, 61, and 66.</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', '287fc208-3e65-4af6-be26-61ff64777a9a', '', '', '2016-06-03 00:00:00', '2016-06-03 00:00:00', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2016/06/03/287fc208-3e65-4af6-be26-61ff64777a9a/', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(1231, 6, '2016-05-23 00:00:00', '2016-05-23 00:00:00', '\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">SALT LAKE CITY—The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAED™) is pleased to announce that Michael Spath has been selected to serve as Chair of the Academy’s Emergency Dispatch Quality Improvement Specialist (ED-Q™) Council of Standards as part of the Academy’s ongoing support of the emergency dispatch quality assurance process. The ED-Q Council of Standards is responsible for the ongoing refinement of the Emergency Dispatch Quality Assurance™ programs.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">At the same time, the Academy would like to thank the council’s former Chair, Brian Dale, for his dedication and efforts to further the ED-Q Standards.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Spath is the Communications Manager for the city of Sunnyvale’s Department of Public Safety in California, USA; he has been a 911 professional since 1991. He is a Master Q Instructor for EPD-Q™, EFD-Q™, and EMD-Q®, and also serves as a member of the Journal of Emergency Dispatch’s Editorial Board.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">When he&#8217;s not working, Spath can be found with his wife, Tammy, and daughter, Emma, at CrossFit West in Santa Cruz—or trying to keep up with them in a Spartan race.</p>\n', 'IAED Announces New Chair of ED-Q Council of Standards', '<p>SALT LAKE CITY—The International Academies of Emergency Dispatch® (IAED™) is pleased to announce that Michael Spath has been selected to serve as Chair of the Academy’s Emergency Dispatch Quality Improvement Specialist (ED-Q™) Council of&#8230;</p>\n', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', '7695eab6-9769-4083-bd7f-1025b931d46f', '', '', '2016-05-23 00:00:00', '2016-05-23 00:00:00', '', 0, 'http://localhost:8888/rnd/2016/05/23/7695eab6-9769-4083-bd7f-1025b931d46f/', 0, 'post', '', 0);
